window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-chemical-coord","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Chemical Coordination and Integration · Practice","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"In the mechanism of action of a protein hormone, one of the second messengers is","image":"","options":["Cyclic AMP","Insulin","T3","Gastrin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Protein (peptide) hormones are not lipid-soluble, so they cannot cross the cell membrane. They bind to receptors on the membrane surface.\nThis hormone–receptor complex causes second messengers to form inside the cell. Examples are cyclic AMP, $IP_3$ and $Ca^{2+}$, and these regulate the cell's metabolism.\nInsulin and gastrin are themselves hormones (first messengers), not second messengers.\n$T_3$ is a thyroid hormone that acts through intracellular receptors.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Impaired synthesis or release of anti-diuretic hormone reduces the kidney's ability to conserve water. This leads to water loss and dehydration. The condition is called:","image":"","options":["Diabetes mellitus","Diabetes insipidus","Addison's disease","Acromegaly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"ADH (vasopressin) helps the kidney conserve water.\nWhen ADH is deficient, water is lost through urine and dehydration follows. This condition is diabetes insipidus.\nDiabetes mellitus is a different disorder. It results from prolonged hyperglycemia, with glucose lost in urine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): After ovulation, progesterone becomes the main hormone secreted by the ovary.\nReason (R): After ovulation, the ruptured graafian follicle is transformed into the corpus luteum, which secretes mainly progesterone.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Before ovulation, the growing follicles secrete mainly estrogen.\nR is true: after ovulation, the ruptured follicle becomes the corpus luteum, which secretes mainly progesterone.\nA is true, and it holds precisely because the corpus luteum, the progesterone source, has now formed.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (action) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\n(a) Androgens\n(b) Estrogens\n(c) Progesterone\n(d) Prolactin\nList-II:\n(i) Formation of alveoli in mammary glands that store milk\n(ii) High pitch of voice\n(iii) Growth of mammary glands and formation of milk\n(iv) Anabolic effects on protein and carbohydrate metabolism","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Androgens produce anabolic (synthetic) effects on protein and carbohydrate metabolism, so a matches iv.\n(b) Estrogens bring out female secondary sex characters, such as a high pitch of voice, so b matches ii.\n(c) Progesterone stimulates the formation of alveoli, the sac-like structures that store milk, so c matches i.\n(d) Prolactin regulates the growth of the mammary glands and milk formation, so d matches iii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the adrenal gland:\n(i) Cortisol is the main glucocorticoid and suppresses the immune response.\n(ii) Aldosterone stimulates the excretion of phosphate ions by the renal tubules.\n(iii) Zona fasciculata is the outermost layer of the adrenal cortex.\n(iv) Catecholamines inhibit the breakdown of lipids and proteins.\n(v) Underproduction of adrenal cortical hormones alters carbohydrate metabolism, causing weakness and fatigue.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (v) only","(i), (iii) and (v) only","(ii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iv) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Cortisol is the main glucocorticoid and it suppresses the immune response.\n(ii) Correct: Aldosterone stimulates the excretion of K$^+$ and phosphate ions.\n(iii) Incorrect: Zona fasciculata is the middle layer; zona glomerulosa is the outermost.\n(iv) Incorrect: Catecholamines stimulate, not inhibit, the breakdown of lipids and proteins.\n(v) Correct: This describes Addison's disease.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the adrenal cortex is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Aldosterone stimulates reabsorption of Na+ and water and excretion of K+ and phosphate ions","Zona reticularis forms the outermost layer of the adrenal cortex","Cortisol produces anti-inflammatory reactions and suppresses the immune response","Underproduction of adrenal cortex hormones causes Addison's disease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: aldosterone acts mainly at the renal tubules in exactly this way.\n(2) Incorrect: the order of layers from outside to inside is zona glomerulosa (outer), zona fasciculata (middle) and zona reticularis (inner).\n(3) Correct: cortisol is anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive.\n(4) Correct: hyposecretion of cortical hormones alters carbohydrate metabolism, causing weakness and fatigue (Addison's disease).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Testosterone, estradiol and progesterone are steroid hormones, which mostly act through intracellular receptors and regulate gene expression.\nStatement II: Unlike androgens, estrogens have no role in regulating sexual behaviour.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT lists testosterone, estradiol and progesterone as steroids. Steroid hormones bind intracellular (mostly nuclear) receptors, and the hormone-receptor complex regulates gene expression.\nStatement II is incorrect. Androgens influence male libido, and estrogens also regulate female sexual behaviour.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly states the location of the human gonads?","image":"","options":["Testes lie in the abdomen; ovaries lie in the pelvic sac outside the abdomen","Testes lie in the scrotal sac outside the abdomen; ovaries lie in the abdomen","Both testes and ovaries lie in the abdominal cavity","Testes lie in the scrotal sac inside the abdomen; ovaries lie above the kidneys"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the pair of testes lies in the scrotal sac, which is outside the abdomen.\nThe pair of ovaries is located in the abdomen.\nOption 3 is wrong because the testes are not abdominal.\nThe structures lying above the kidneys are the adrenal glands, not the ovaries.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pituitary hormone) with List-II (action):\nList-I:\n(a) Prolactin\n(b) TSH\n(c) ACTH\n(d) MSH\nList-II:\n(i) Synthesis and secretion of glucocorticoids\n(ii) Regulation of skin pigmentation\n(iii) Growth of mammary glands and milk formation\n(iv) Synthesis and secretion of thyroid hormones","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Prolactin regulates mammary gland growth and milk formation, so a-iii.\n(b) TSH stimulates the thyroid to make and release its hormones, so b-iv.\n(c) ACTH stimulates the adrenal cortex to secrete glucocorticoids, so c-i.\n(d) MSH acts on melanocytes and regulates pigmentation, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"In the human thyroid gland, the two lobes on either side of the trachea are joined by a thin flap of connective tissue. What is this flap called?","image":"","options":["Stroma","Isthmus","Follicle","Sella tursica"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The thyroid has two lobes, one on each side of the trachea.\nA thin flap of connective tissue called the isthmus joins the two lobes.\nStroma is the supporting tissue lying between the follicles, not the bridge between the lobes.\nThe sella tursica is the bony cavity that holds the pituitary.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Parathyroid hormone is described as a hypercalcemic hormone.\nReason (R): Parathyroid hormone stimulates reabsorption of Ca²⁺ by the renal tubules and increases Ca²⁺ absorption from digested food.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT calls PTH a hypercalcemic hormone because it increases blood Ca²⁺.\nR is true: PTH increases renal tubular reabsorption of Ca²⁺ and intestinal absorption of Ca²⁺.\nBoth of these actions add Ca²⁺ to the blood, so R explains why PTH is hypercalcemic.\nBone resorption is a third such action, but it is not needed for R to be a valid explanation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The immune responses of old persons become weak.\nReason (R): Thymosins help only in humoral immunity by promoting antibody production and have no role in T-lymphocyte differentiation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The thymus degenerates in old individuals, so less thymosin is produced and immune responses weaken.\nR is false. Thymosins play a major role in the differentiation of T-lymphocytes, which provide cell-mediated immunity. They also promote antibody production for humoral immunity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Glucose transported by blood to distant cells is not a hormone, since hormones are non-nutrient chemicals","The neural system and the endocrine system jointly coordinate and regulate physiological functions","Liver, kidney and heart produce hormones even though they are not organised endocrine bodies","Because hormones must act on distantly located organs, they are produced in large amounts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: under the current definition, hormones are non-nutrient chemicals, so a nutrient like glucose is excluded even though blood carries it.\n(2) Correct: NCERT says the two systems jointly coordinate and regulate body functions.\n(3) Correct: NCERT lists the GI tract, liver, kidney and heart as other hormone-producing organs.\n(4) Incorrect: hormones are produced in trace amounts. In addition, the current definition does not require action on distant organs.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A person loses large amounts of water in urine and becomes dehydrated because the kidney cannot conserve water. Impaired synthesis or release of which hormone causes this condition?","image":"","options":["Insulin, causing diabetes mellitus","Vasopressin, causing diabetes insipidus","Growth hormone, causing acromegaly","Oxytocin, causing diabetes insipidus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Vasopressin (ADH) stimulates the distal tubules to reabsorb water and electrolytes, which reduces diuresis.\nIf ADH synthesis or release is impaired, the kidney cannot conserve water. Water loss and dehydration follow. This condition is diabetes insipidus.\nDiabetes mellitus is the tempting choice, but it involves insulin and glucose loss in urine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Blood glucose level rises during a sudden emergency such as fright.\nReason (R): Catecholamines secreted in stress stimulate the breakdown of glycogen.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In emergencies the adrenal medulla rapidly releases adrenaline and noradrenaline, and blood glucose rises.\nR is true. Catecholamines stimulate glycogen breakdown (glycogenolysis), which increases the concentration of glucose in blood.\nR therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which gastrointestinal hormone inhibits gastric secretion and motility?","image":"","options":["Gastrin","Gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP)","Secretin","Cholecystokinin (CCK)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Among the four major GI peptide hormones, only gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP) inhibits gastric secretion and motility.\nGastrin does the opposite: it stimulates the gastric glands.\nSecretin acts on the pancreas, and CCK acts on the pancreas and gall bladder.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (gastrointestinal hormone) with List-II (action):\nList-I\n(a) Gastrin\n(b) Secretin\n(c) Cholecystokinin\n(d) Gastric inhibitory peptide\nList-II\n(i) Stimulates secretion of water and bicarbonate ions from exocrine pancreas\n(ii) Inhibits gastric secretion and motility\n(iii) Stimulates secretion of HCl and pepsinogen from gastric glands\n(iv) Stimulates secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Gastrin acts on the gastric glands and stimulates secretion of HCl and pepsinogen, so (a) matches (iii).\n(b) Secretin acts on the exocrine pancreas and stimulates release of water and bicarbonate ions, so (b) matches (i).\n(c) CCK acts on both the pancreas and the gall bladder, releasing pancreatic enzymes and bile juice, so (c) matches (iv).\n(d) GIP inhibits gastric secretion and motility, so (d) matches (ii).\nOption (2) swaps secretin and CCK, which is a common confusion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The immune responses of old persons become weak.\nReason (R): The thymus degenerates in old individuals, resulting in decreased production of thymosins.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that immune responses weaken in old age.\nR is true: the thymus degenerates with age, so less thymosin is produced.\nR explains A: fewer thymosins means poorer T-lymphocyte differentiation and less antibody production, so immunity weakens.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which hormone is secreted by the pineal gland?","image":"","options":["Melanocyte stimulating hormone","Melanin","Melatonin","Thymosin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The pineal gland secretes melatonin, which regulates the body's 24-hour (diurnal) rhythms.\nMSH is a hormone, but it comes from the pars intermedia of the pituitary.\nMelanin is a skin pigment found in melanocytes. It is not a hormone.\nThymosins are secreted by the thymus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Neural coordination\n(b) Hormonal coordination\n(c) Invertebrates\n(d) Vertebrates\nList-II\n(i) Very simple endocrine system with few hormones\n(ii) Point-to-point, rapid but short-lived\n(iii) Large number of chemicals act as hormones\n(iv) Continuous regulation of cellular functions, including cells not innervated by nerves","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Neural coordination is point-to-point, rapid and short-lived, so a matches (ii).\n(b) Hormonal coordination continuously regulates cells that nerves do not reach, so b matches (iv).\n(c) Invertebrates have a very simple endocrine system with few hormones, so c matches (i).\n(d) In vertebrates, many chemicals act as hormones, so d matches (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Choose the correct option among the following options\nColumn I — Column II\nA. Epinephrine — i. Stimulates muscle growth\nB. Testosterone — ii. Decrease in blood pressure\nC. Glucagon — iii. Breakdown of liver glycogen content\nD. Atrial natriuretic factor — iv. Increases heart beat","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-i","A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-i, B-iv, C-ii, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Epinephrine increases heart beat and the strength of contraction → iv.\nB. Testosterone has anabolic effects, stimulating muscle growth → i.\nC. Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis, breaking down liver glycogen → iii.\nD. Atrial natriuretic factor causes vasodilation, which decreases blood pressure → ii.\nThe correct match is A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii, which is option (2).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q12)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A hormone responsible for normal sleep-wake cycle is","image":"","options":["Epinephrine","Gastrin","Melatonin","Insulin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: the pineal gland secretes melatonin.\nMelatonin regulates the 24-hour (diurnal) rhythm, including the normal sleep-wake cycle and body temperature. It also influences metabolism, pigmentation and the menstrual cycle.\nEpinephrine is an emergency (fight-or-flight) hormone. Gastrin stimulates gastric juice secretion. Insulin lowers blood glucose. None of these controls the sleep-wake cycle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Endocrine glands are also called ductless glands because they:","image":"","options":["Lack ducts to carry their secretions","Release their secretions only into body cavities","Secrete only enzymes and no hormones","Are always attached to a nerve fibre"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that endocrine glands lack ducts, which is why they are called ductless glands. Their secretions are hormones.\nOption (2) is wrong because release into body cavities does not define these glands; the defining feature is the absence of ducts.\nOption (3) is wrong because enzymes reaching a site through a duct describe exocrine glands.\nOption (4) is wrong because nerve attachment is not the basis of the name.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Glucagon is a peptide hormone","Diabetes mellitus is associated with the formation of harmful ketone bodies","Glucagon increases cellular glucose uptake and utilisation","Diabetic patients can be successfully treated with insulin therapy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT states that glucagon is a peptide hormone.\n(2) Correct: diabetes mellitus involves loss of glucose in urine and the formation of ketone bodies.\n(3) Incorrect: glucagon reduces cellular glucose uptake and utilisation. Increasing uptake is the action of insulin.\n(4) Correct: diabetic patients are successfully treated with insulin therapy.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A child has a hypothalamic disorder that causes persistent over-secretion of somatostatin. All other glands are normal. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["Gigantism due to excess growth hormone","Stunted growth resembling pituitary dwarfism","Acromegaly with disfigurement of the face","Diabetes insipidus due to loss of ADH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Somatostatin is a hypothalamic inhibiting hormone that suppresses growth hormone release from the pituitary.\nToo much somatostatin therefore means too little GH.\nIn a child, low GH causes stunted growth, the picture seen in pituitary dwarfism.\nGigantism and acromegaly result from excess GH, which is the opposite effect.\nDiabetes insipidus involves ADH, not somatostatin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Aldosterone helps in the maintenance of blood pressure.\nReason (R): Aldosterone stimulates reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water by the renal tubules.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: aldosterone acts mainly at the renal tubules and stimulates reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water.\nIt also promotes excretion of K$^+$ and phosphate.\nA is true: retaining Na$^+$ and water maintains body fluid volume and osmotic pressure, and therefore blood pressure.\nSo R is the mechanism behind A, and it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the mechanism of hormone action is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Each hormone receptor is specific to only one hormone","Formation of the hormone-receptor complex brings about biochemical changes in the target tissue","Pituitary and hypothalamic hormones belong to the steroid group and therefore act on nuclear receptors","IP$_3$ and Ca$^{2+}$ can act as second messengers for hormones binding membrane-bound receptors"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: receptors are specific, with each receptor binding one hormone only.\n(2) Correct: the hormone-receptor complex triggers biochemical changes that regulate target-tissue metabolism.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT classifies pituitary and hypothalamic hormones as peptide/polypeptide/protein hormones, not steroids. They act via membrane-bound receptors.\n(4) Correct: cAMP, IP$_3$ and Ca$^{2+}$ are listed as second messengers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about anterior pituitary hormones:\n(i) TSH stimulates synthesis and secretion of thyroid hormones.\n(ii) ACTH stimulates secretion of glucocorticoids from the adrenal cortex.\n(iii) ACTH stimulates secretion of adrenaline from the adrenal medulla.\n(iv) FSH stimulates growth and development of ovarian follicles.\n(v) Prolactin causes milk ejection during suckling.\n(vi) In males, LH stimulates secretion of androgens from the testis.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii), (iii) and (vi) only","(i), (ii), (iv) and (vi) only","(ii), (iv), (v) and (vi) only","(i), (iii), (iv) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: TSH acts on the thyroid.\n(ii) Correct: ACTH stimulates glucocorticoids, which are steroid hormones from the adrenal cortex.\n(iii) Incorrect: ACTH acts on the cortex, not the medulla. The medulla secretes catecholamines.\n(iv) Correct: FSH acts on ovarian follicles.\n(v) Incorrect: oxytocin causes milk ejection. Prolactin regulates milk formation.\n(vi) Correct: LH stimulates androgen secretion in males.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"In a patient, the $\\beta$-cells of the Islets of Langerhans have been destroyed, while the $\\alpha$-cells remain functional. If the patient is left untreated, which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["Blood glucose falls sharply because glucagon now acts without opposition","Hepatocytes store more glycogen because glycogenesis is no longer opposed","Prolonged hyperglycemia with glucose loss in urine and ketone body formation, which can be managed by insulin therapy","Adipocytes rapidly take up blood glucose, causing hypoglycemia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Destroying the $\\beta$-cells stops insulin secretion.\nWithout insulin, hepatocytes and adipocytes take up less glucose and glycogenesis falls. Options (2) and (4) are therefore wrong.\nGlucagon from the intact $\\alpha$-cells keeps driving glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Blood sugar rises, not falls, so option (1) is wrong.\nThe resulting prolonged hyperglycemia is diabetes mellitus, with glucose in urine and ketone bodies. It is treated with insulin therapy.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are organised endocrine bodies in the human body?\nPituitary, Thymus, Liver, Kidney, Pineal, Heart, Parathyroid","image":"","options":["3","5","6","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the organised endocrine bodies as pituitary, pineal, thyroid, adrenal, pancreas, parathyroid, thymus and gonads.\nFrom the given list, the organised endocrine bodies are pituitary, thymus, pineal and parathyroid.\nLiver, kidney and heart do produce hormones, but they are not organised endocrine glands.\nSo the count is 4.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (chemical class):\nList-I\n(a) Glucagon\n(b) Testosterone\n(c) Triiodothyronine\n(d) Adrenaline\nList-II\n(i) Iodothyronine\n(ii) Amino-acid derivative\n(iii) Peptide\n(iv) Steroid","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Glucagon is a peptide hormone, so a-iii.\n(b) Testosterone is a steroid, so b-iv.\n(c) Triiodothyronine is a thyroid hormone, which is an iodothyronine, so c-i.\n(d) Adrenaline (epinephrine) is an amino-acid derivative, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Both catecholamines and glucocorticoids can raise blood glucose; catecholamines do so by stimulating glycogen breakdown, while glucocorticoids stimulate gluconeogenesis.\nStatement II: Glucocorticoids enhance the cellular uptake and utilisation of amino acids.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Catecholamines stimulate breakdown of glycogen, which raises blood glucose.\nGlucocorticoids stimulate gluconeogenesis, along with lipolysis and proteolysis.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that glucocorticoids inhibit cellular uptake and utilisation of amino acids.\nThis inhibition spares amino acids so they can be used for gluconeogenesis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are regulated or influenced by melatonin, according to NCERT?\n(i) Sleep-wake cycle\n(ii) Rhythm of body temperature\n(iii) Pigmentation\n(iv) Menstrual cycle\n(v) Blood $Ca^{2+}$ level\n(vi) Defense capability\n(vii) Milk ejection from mammary glands","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i), (ii) Correct: melatonin maintains the sleep-wake and body temperature rhythms.\n(iii), (iv), (vi) Correct: it also influences pigmentation, the menstrual cycle and defense capability.\n(v) Incorrect: blood calcium is controlled by PTH and TCT.\n(vii) Incorrect: milk ejection is caused by oxytocin.\nTotal correct = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Insulin and glucagon are both peptide hormones. Based on their chemical nature, which statement correctly describes how they act on their target cells?","image":"","options":["They enter the nucleus and bind nuclear receptors to directly regulate gene expression","They bind membrane-bound receptors and generally do not enter the cell, acting through second messengers","They bind intracellular receptors in the cytoplasm, like steroid hormones","They act on all body cells equally, without needing any specific receptors"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Peptide hormones such as insulin and glucagon bind specific membrane-bound receptors on target cells.\nSuch hormones normally do not enter the target cell.\nInstead, they generate second messengers such as cAMP, $IP_3$ and $Ca^{2+}$, which regulate cellular metabolism.\nIntracellular or nuclear receptors are used by steroid hormones and iodothyronines, so options (1) and (3) are wrong.\nReceptors are always specific, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following hormones is not secreted by anterior pituitary?","image":"","options":["Growth hormone","Follicle stimulating hormone","Oxytocin","Adrenocorticotrophic hormone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) secretes GH, PRL, TSH, ACTH, LH, FSH and MSH.\nOxytocin is made by hypothalamic neurons. It travels down their axons and is stored in and released from the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis).\nSo oxytocin is the hormone not secreted by the anterior pituitary.\nGrowth hormone, FSH and ACTH are all anterior pituitary hormones, so options (1), (2) and (4) do not fit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) $\\alpha$-cells\n(b) $\\beta$-cells\n(c) Hepatocytes and adipocytes\n(d) Ketone bodies\nList-II\n(i) Main target cells of insulin\n(ii) Secrete glucagon\n(iii) Harmful compounds formed in diabetes mellitus\n(iv) Secrete insulin","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $\\alpha$-cells secrete glucagon, so a-ii.\n(b) $\\beta$-cells secrete insulin, so b-iv.\n(c) Insulin acts mainly on hepatocytes and adipocytes, increasing their glucose uptake, so c-i.\n(d) Prolonged hyperglycemia (diabetes mellitus) leads to glucose loss in urine and the formation of harmful ketone bodies, so d-iii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (gastrointestinal hormone) with List-II (action):\nList-I\n(a) Gastrin\n(b) Secretin\n(c) Cholecystokinin\n(d) Gastric inhibitory peptide\nList-II\n(i) Inhibits gastric secretion and motility\n(ii) Stimulates secretion of HCl and pepsinogen\n(iii) Stimulates secretion of water and bicarbonate ions from exocrine pancreas\n(iv) Stimulates secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Gastrin acts on the gastric glands and stimulates secretion of HCl and pepsinogen, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) Secretin acts on the exocrine pancreas and stimulates secretion of water and bicarbonate ions, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) CCK acts on both the pancreas and the gall bladder, releasing pancreatic enzymes and bile juice, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) GIP inhibits gastric secretion and motility, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following acts as a second messenger when a peptide hormone binds to a membrane-bound receptor on its target cell?","image":"","options":["Cyclic AMP","Cortisol","Messenger RNA","Thyroxine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Hormones that bind membrane-bound receptors usually stay outside the cell.\nThe binding generates second messengers inside the cell, such as cyclic AMP, IP$_3$ and Ca$^{2+}$. These then regulate cellular metabolism.\nCortisol and thyroxine are themselves hormones that use intracellular receptors.\nmRNA is a product of gene expression, not a second messenger.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The pars intermedia secretes only melanocyte stimulating hormone and in humans is almost merged with the pars distalis.\nStatement II: Like the anterior pituitary, the posterior pituitary is regulated by hypothalamic hormones that reach it through a portal circulatory system.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The pars intermedia secretes only MSH, and in humans it is almost merged with the pars distalis.\nStatement II is incorrect. The portal circulation carries hypothalamic hormones only to the anterior pituitary. The posterior pituitary is under direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Addison's disease, which shows acute weakness and fatigue, results from:","image":"","options":["Overproduction of adrenaline by the adrenal medulla","Underproduction of hormones by the adrenal cortex","Excess secretion of aldosterone","Underproduction of thymosins by the thymus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT links Addison's disease to underproduction of hormones by the adrenal cortex.\nThis deficiency alters carbohydrate metabolism, which causes acute weakness and fatigue.\nThe disease involves a deficiency of cortical hormones, not an excess of any hormone. It is also not related to the medulla or the thymus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which hormone secreted by the thyroid gland is a protein hormone that regulates blood calcium levels?","image":"","options":["Thyroxine ($T_4$)","Triiodothyronine ($T_3$)","Parathyroid hormone","Thyrocalcitonin (TCT)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Besides $T_3$ and $T_4$, the thyroid secretes a protein hormone called thyrocalcitonin (TCT).\nTCT regulates blood calcium by lowering it.\n$T_3$ and $T_4$ are iodothyronines that mainly regulate BMR and metabolism.\nPTH also regulates calcium, but it is made by the parathyroid glands and raises blood $Ca^{2+}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following hormones is released into the blood by a gland that does not synthesise it?","image":"","options":["Melanocyte stimulating hormone","Prolactin","Vasopressin","Melatonin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Vasopressin (ADH) and oxytocin are made by hypothalamic neurons.\nThey travel down the axons to the neurohypophysis, which stores and releases them.\nMSH comes from the pars intermedia, prolactin from the pars distalis and melatonin from the pineal gland. Each of these glands makes its own hormone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In females, LH induces ovulation of fully mature Graafian follicles and maintains the corpus luteum.\nStatement II: In males, FSH alone, without any role of androgens, regulates spermatogenesis.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: LH triggers ovulation of the Graafian follicle and maintains the corpus luteum formed from its remnants.\nStatement II is incorrect: spermatogenesis is regulated by FSH together with androgens.\nIn males, LH stimulates the testis to produce those androgens.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"In the human testis, the androgens (mainly testosterone) are produced by which cells?","image":"","options":["Sertoli cells lining the seminiferous tubules","Leydig cells present in the intertubular spaces","Spermatogonia present in the seminiferous tubules","Cells of the epididymis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The testis has seminiferous tubules and stromal (interstitial) tissue.\nThe Leydig cells, also called interstitial cells, lie in the spaces between the tubules. They produce androgens, mainly testosterone.\nSertoli cells (option 1) are a tempting choice because they lie close to the developing sperms. However, NCERT credits androgen secretion to the Leydig cells.\nSpermatogonia are germ cells, and the epididymis is an accessory duct. Neither of them is an androgen source.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (GI tract hormone) with List-II (action):\nList-I\n(a) Gastrin\n(b) Secretin\n(c) Cholecystokinin\n(d) Gastric inhibitory peptide\nList-II\n(i) Inhibits gastric secretion and motility\n(ii) Secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice\n(iii) Secretion of HCl and pepsinogen\n(iv) Secretion of water and bicarbonate ions by exocrine pancreas","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Gastrin acts on gastric glands, causing secretion of HCl and pepsinogen: a-iii.\n(b) Secretin makes the exocrine pancreas release water and bicarbonate: b-iv.\n(c) CCK acts on both the pancreas and the gall bladder, releasing enzymes and bile: c-ii.\n(d) GIP inhibits gastric secretion and motility: d-i.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Hypothyroidism in a pregnant woman can lead to stunted growth and mental retardation in her baby.\nReason (R): Thyroid hormones are needed for the normal development and maturation of the growing baby, including its central neural system.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: hypothyroidism during pregnancy causes cretinism in the baby. Its features include stunted growth, mental retardation, low IQ, abnormal skin and deaf-mutism.\nR is true: thyroid hormones regulate the development and maturation of the body, including the central neural system.\nR explains A, because lack of these hormones disturbs the baby's development.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the adrenal gland is correct?","image":"","options":["Zona glomerulosa is the innermost layer of the adrenal cortex","Aldosterone stimulates reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water and excretion of K$^+$ and phosphate ions at the renal tubules","Cortisol suppresses the immune response and inhibits RBC production","Small amounts of androgenic steroids are secreted by the adrenal medulla"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: From inside outward the layers are zona reticularis, zona fasciculata and zona glomerulosa, so glomerulosa is the outer layer.\n(2) Correct: This is the NCERT description of aldosterone's action, which maintains fluid volume and blood pressure.\n(3) Wrong: Cortisol does suppress the immune response, but it stimulates RBC production.\n(4) Wrong: The small amounts of androgens come from the adrenal cortex, not the medulla.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the pituitary gland:\n(i) In humans, the pars intermedia is almost merged with the pars distalis.\n(ii) Acromegaly is easily detected at an early stage, because facial changes appear soon after GH excess begins.\n(iii) Oxytocin acts on smooth muscles and stimulates their contraction.\n(iv) Vasopressin stimulates resorption of water and electrolytes mainly by the proximal convoluted tubules.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (iii) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(iii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in humans the pars intermedia is almost merged with the pars distalis.\n(ii) Incorrect: acromegaly is hard to diagnose early. It often goes undetected for years until external features change.\n(iii) Correct: oxytocin contracts smooth muscle, for example the uterus during childbirth.\n(iv) Incorrect: vasopressin acts on the distal tubules, not the proximal tubules.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Gonadotrophin releasing hormone (GnRH) from the hypothalamus stimulates the pituitary to synthesise and release","image":"","options":["oxytocin and vasopressin","TSH and ACTH","LH and FSH","GH and prolactin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"GnRH is a releasing hormone that stimulates the pituitary to synthesise and release gonadotrophins.\nThe gonadotrophins are LH and FSH, which act on the gonads.\nOxytocin and vasopressin are not secreted in response to releasing hormones. They are hypothalamic products stored in the posterior pituitary.\nTSH, ACTH, GH and prolactin are anterior pituitary hormones, but they are not gonadotrophins.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Oxytocin and vasopressin are synthesised by the cells of the posterior pituitary.\nStatement II: The posterior pituitary is under the direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Oxytocin and vasopressin are synthesised by the hypothalamus. They are carried along axons to the neurohypophysis, which only stores and releases them.\nStatement II is correct. The posterior pituitary is directly regulated by hypothalamic neurons. The anterior pituitary, in contrast, is regulated through a portal circulation.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Erythropoietin, a hormone that stimulates formation of red blood cells, is produced by","image":"","options":["Leydig cells of the testis","Hepatocytes of the liver","Juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney","Alpha-cells of the islets of Langerhans"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney produce the peptide hormone erythropoietin.\nErythropoietin stimulates erythropoiesis, which is the formation of RBCs.\nLeydig cells secrete androgens. Alpha-cells secrete glucagon. The liver is not the source named for this hormone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. $\\alpha$-cells\nB. $\\beta$-cells\nC. Hepatocytes\nD. Ketone bodies\nList-II\n(i) Secrete insulin\n(ii) Harmful compounds formed in diabetes mellitus\n(iii) Secrete glucagon\n(iv) Main target cells of glucagon","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): $\\alpha$-cells secrete glucagon.\nB-(i): $\\beta$-cells secrete insulin.\nC-(iv): glucagon acts mainly on liver cells (hepatocytes).\nD-(ii): ketone bodies are harmful compounds formed in diabetes mellitus.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which layer of the adrenal cortex lies outermost, just beneath the capsule?","image":"","options":["Zona reticularis","Zona fasciculata","Zona glomerulosa","Adrenal medulla"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT divides the adrenal cortex into three layers, listed from inside to outside.\nInner layer: zona reticularis, which lies next to the medulla.\nMiddle layer: zona fasciculata.\nOuter layer: zona glomerulosa.\nThe adrenal medulla is the central tissue, so it cannot be the outermost layer of the cortex.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"The secretion of parathyroid hormone is regulated mainly by:","image":"","options":["Thyroid stimulating hormone from the anterior pituitary","Circulating levels of calcium ions in blood","A releasing hormone from the hypothalamus","The level of iodine in the diet"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that PTH secretion is regulated by the circulating levels of calcium ions.\nTSH controls the thyroid gland, not the parathyroids.\nNo hypothalamic releasing hormone for PTH is described.\nIodine is needed for making thyroid hormones, not PTH.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The follicular cells of the thyroid gland, which enclose a cavity, synthesise tetraiodothyronine ($T_4$) and triiodothyronine ($T_3$).\nStatement II: Iodine is essential for the normal rate of thyroid hormone synthesis.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Each thyroid follicle is made of follicular cells around a cavity, and these cells make $T_4$ (thyroxine) and $T_3$.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that iodine is essential for the normal rate of thyroid hormone synthesis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Where is the pineal gland located in the human body?","image":"","options":["On the ventral side of the forebrain, as the basal part of the diencephalon","On the dorsal side of the forebrain","In a bony cavity called sella tursica","Behind the sternum on the ventral side of the aorta"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the pineal gland lies on the dorsal side of the forebrain.\nThe basal part of the diencephalon is the hypothalamus, not the pineal gland.\nThe pituitary sits in the sella tursica.\nThe thymus lies behind the sternum on the ventral side of the aorta.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"In a lactating mother, which pituitary hormone causes milk ejection from the mammary glands?","image":"","options":["Prolactin","Luteinizing hormone","Oxytocin","Melanocyte stimulating hormone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Oxytocin stimulates contraction of smooth muscles. In females it causes vigorous uterine contraction at childbirth and milk ejection from the mammary gland.\nProlactin is the tempting option, but it regulates mammary gland growth and milk formation, not ejection.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"PTH stimulates 'bone resorption'. In this context, bone resorption refers to:","image":"","options":["Dissolution or demineralisation of bone","Deposition of calcium in the bone matrix","Formation of new red blood cells in bone marrow","Growth in length of long bones"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT explains bone resorption as the dissolution or demineralisation of bone.\nThis process releases Ca²⁺ from bone into the blood, which raises the blood calcium level.\nDeposition of calcium in bone is the opposite process and would lower blood Ca²⁺.\nThe other two options describe red blood cell formation and bone growth, which are unrelated to this action of PTH.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of androgens in human males?\nMuscular growth; high pitch of voice; aggressiveness; anabolic effect on protein metabolism; formation of mammary alveoli; libido; growth of axillary hair","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Androgen effects in the list:\n- muscular growth\n- aggressiveness\n- anabolic effect on protein metabolism\n- libido\n- growth of axillary hair\nThis gives 5 effects.\nThe other two are not androgen effects:\n- A high pitch of voice is caused by estrogen.\n- Formation of mammary alveoli is caused by progesterone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about androgens is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["They regulate the development and functions of the epididymis, vas deferens and prostate gland","They act on the central neural system and influence male libido","They produce catabolic effects that break down proteins and carbohydrates","They play a major stimulatory role in spermatogenesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: androgens regulate the development, maturation and functions of the male accessory sex organs.\n(2) Correct: they act on the CNS and influence male sexual behaviour (libido).\n(3) Incorrect: androgens have anabolic (synthetic) effects on protein and carbohydrate metabolism, not catabolic effects.\n(4) Correct: they strongly stimulate spermatogenesis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes are stimulated by glucagon?\n(i) Glycogenolysis\n(ii) Gluconeogenesis\n(iii) Glycogenesis\n(iv) Cellular glucose uptake and utilisation","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Stimulated: Glucagon acts on hepatocytes and causes glycogenolysis.\n(ii) Stimulated: Glucagon promotes gluconeogenesis, which adds to hyperglycemia.\n(iii) Not stimulated: Glycogenesis is promoted by insulin.\n(iv) Not stimulated: Glucagon reduces cellular glucose uptake and utilisation; insulin enhances it.\nThe count is therefore 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Two adult women, X and Y, both have a visibly enlarged thyroid gland. X lives in a region with iodine-poor soil and water, and reports irregular menstrual cycles and a lowered metabolic rate. Y has protruding eyeballs, an increased basal metabolic rate and unexplained weight loss. Which interpretation is correct?","image":"","options":["X has hyperthyroidism caused by thyroid nodules; Y has simple goitre","X has goitre with hypothyroidism; Y has exophthalmic goitre, a form of hyperthyroidism","Both X and Y have hypothyroidism, since both show thyroid enlargement","X has Graves' disease; Y has cretinism caused by iodine deficiency"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An enlarged thyroid occurs in both low and high hormone states, so enlargement alone cannot identify the condition.\nX: Iodine deficiency reduces $T_3$/$T_4$ synthesis, causing hypothyroidism with goitre. A low metabolic rate and irregular menses in adult women fit this picture.\nY: Bulging eyes, high BMR and weight loss define exophthalmic goitre (Graves' disease), which is a form of hyperthyroidism.\nOption (3) fails because Y's raised BMR indicates excess hormone. Cretinism (option 4) affects a developing baby, not an adult.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A patient is given high doses of cortisol for a long period. Based on the actions of glucocorticoids, which combination of effects is most likely?","image":"","options":["Fall in blood glucose and stronger inflammatory responses","Rise in blood glucose and weakened immune response","Increased cellular uptake of amino acids and reduced RBC count","Retention of K$^+$ and increased loss of Na$^+$ in urine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cortisol stimulates gluconeogenesis, which makes new glucose. Blood glucose therefore tends to rise.\nCortisol also produces anti-inflammatory reactions and suppresses the immune response, so immunity is weakened.\nOption (1) is the opposite of both effects.\nOption (3) is wrong because cortisol inhibits amino acid uptake and stimulates RBC production.\nOption (4) describes an effect opposite to aldosterone, not cortisol.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A hormone that lowers blood pressure by causing dilation of blood vessels is secreted by:","image":"","options":["The atrial wall of the heart","Juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney","The zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex","The posterior pituitary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The atrial wall secretes atrial natriuretic factor (ANF) when blood pressure rises.\nANF dilates blood vessels and so lowers blood pressure.\nJuxtaglomerular cells produce erythropoietin, which stimulates RBC formation.\nAldosterone and vasopressin promote water retention and do not lower blood pressure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hormones made by hypothalamic neurons reach the anterior pituitary through a portal circulatory system.\nStatement II: The posterior pituitary is regulated by the hypothalamus through the same portal circulatory system.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Releasing and inhibiting hormones leave the nerve endings of hypothalamic neurons and travel through portal circulation to the anterior pituitary.\nStatement II is incorrect. The posterior pituitary is under direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus, not portal blood control.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A patient's hypothalamic axons ending in the pars nervosa are damaged, but the anterior pituitary still functions normally. Which combination of effects is most likely?","image":"","options":["Excretion of large volumes of glucose-rich urine and reduced milk production","Excretion of large volumes of dilute urine, and weak milk ejection during lactation","Stunted growth and failure of ovulation","Reduced urine output and excessive uterine contractions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The pars nervosa releases oxytocin and vasopressin. Both reach it along axons from the hypothalamus.\nLosing vasopressin (ADH) means less water is resorbed in the distal tubules. Large volumes of dilute urine result (diabetes insipidus).\nLosing oxytocin weakens milk ejection and uterine contraction.\nGlucose in urine indicates diabetes mellitus, not a pituitary cause.\nGrowth and ovulation depend on GH and LH from the intact anterior pituitary.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"On the basis of chemical nature, thymosins secreted by the thymus are:","image":"","options":["Steroid hormones","Peptide hormones","Iodothyronines","Amino-acid derivatives like catecholamines"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes thymosins as peptide hormones secreted by the thymus.\nSteroids include cortisol and testosterone.\nIodothyronines are the thyroid hormones.\nEpinephrine is an example of an amino-acid derivative.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Corpus luteum secretes a hormone called","image":"","options":["Prolactin","Progesterone","Aldosterone","Testosterone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"After ovulation, the ruptured Graafian follicle changes into a structure called the corpus luteum.\nThe corpus luteum secretes mainly progesterone. Progesterone maintains the endometrium and so supports pregnancy.\nProlactin is a hormone of the anterior pituitary.\nAldosterone comes from the adrenal cortex.\nTestosterone comes from the Leydig cells of the testis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"In the human testis, the hormone-producing Leydig cells are located in the:","image":"","options":["lumen of the seminiferous tubules","intertubular spaces between the seminiferous tubules","wall of the epididymis","tunica covering the scrotal sac"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The testis is made of seminiferous tubules and stromal (interstitial) tissue.\nLeydig cells, also called interstitial cells, lie in the intertubular spaces, which is the stromal tissue between the tubules.\nThey produce androgens, mainly testosterone.\nThe tubule lumen is where spermatozoa are formed, not where androgens are made.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A section of the gland that sits above each kidney shows a centrally located tissue surrounded by an outer tissue. The central tissue secretes:","image":"","options":["Cortisol and aldosterone","Androgenic steroids","Glucocorticoids only","Adrenaline and noradrenaline"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The gland described is the adrenal gland.\nIts central part is the adrenal medulla, which secretes the catecholamines adrenaline and noradrenaline.\nCortisol, aldosterone and the small amounts of androgenic steroids all come from the outer adrenal cortex. The tempting cortical options therefore do not fit.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glucagon normally does not need to enter its target liver cell to bring about its effect.\nReason (R): Glucagon is a peptide hormone that binds a membrane-bound receptor, and the resulting second messengers regulate cellular metabolism.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: hormones that interact with membrane-bound receptors normally do not enter the target cell.\nR is true: glucagon is a peptide hormone, and binding to its surface receptor generates second messengers such as cAMP, which alter metabolism inside the cell.\nR explains why entry into the cell is unnecessary.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Aldosterone acts on the renal tubules and stimulates excretion of Na$^+$ and reabsorption of K$^+$.\nStatement II: Aldosterone helps in maintaining body fluid volume, osmotic pressure and blood pressure.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect because it reverses the ions. Aldosterone stimulates reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water, and excretion of K$^+$ and phosphate ions.\nStatement II is correct. Through this ion and water balance, aldosterone maintains electrolytes, body fluid volume, osmotic pressure and blood pressure.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (action):\nList-I\n(a) Gastrin\n(b) Secretin\n(c) Cholecystokinin\n(d) Gastric inhibitory peptide\nList-II\n(i) Secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice\n(ii) Inhibition of gastric secretion and motility\n(iii) Secretion of HCl and pepsinogen\n(iv) Secretion of water and bicarbonate ions from exocrine pancreas","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Gastrin acts on gastric glands to release HCl and pepsinogen, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Secretin acts on the exocrine pancreas to release water and bicarbonate ions, so it matches (iv).\n(c) CCK stimulates secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice, so it matches (i).\n(d) GIP inhibits gastric secretion and motility, so it matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Steroid hormones mostly regulate gene expression or chromosome function in target cells.\nReason (R): Steroid hormones bind to membrane-bound receptors and generate second messengers such as cyclic AMP.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Steroid hormones act through intracellular, mostly nuclear, receptors. The hormone-receptor complex interacts with the genome and regulates gene expression.\nR is false. Generating second messengers such as cAMP, IP$_3$ and Ca$^{2+}$ is typical of hormones that bind membrane-bound receptors, for example protein hormones.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["In females, FSH induces ovulation and maintains the corpus luteum","The corpus luteum formed after ovulation secretes mainly estrogen","In males, LH stimulates secretion of androgens from the testis, while FSH and androgens regulate spermatogenesis","Somatostatin from the hypothalamus stimulates the release of growth hormone from the pituitary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: LH induces ovulation of Graafian follicles and maintains the corpus luteum. FSH stimulates follicle growth.\n(2) Incorrect: the corpus luteum secretes mainly progesterone. Estrogen comes mainly from growing follicles.\n(3) Correct: LH acts on the Leydig cells of the testis to release androgens, and FSH together with androgens regulates spermatogenesis.\n(4) Incorrect: somatostatin inhibits GH release.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about exophthalmic goitre is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It is a form of hyperthyroidism.","It is characterised by protrusion of the eyeballs.","It is accompanied by a decreased basal metabolic rate and weight gain.","It is also called Graves' disease."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: exophthalmic goitre is a form of hyperthyroidism.\n(2) Correct: protrusion of the eyeballs is a characteristic feature.\n(3) Incorrect: excess thyroid hormone increases the BMR and causes weight loss, not weight gain.\n(4) Correct: the condition is also called Graves' disease.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about thyroid-related conditions is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Dietary iodine deficiency can cause hypothyroidism with enlargement of the thyroid gland","Hypothyroidism during pregnancy may cause cretinism in the baby, with low IQ and deaf-mutism","Exophthalmic goitre is marked by protruding eyeballs, decreased basal metabolic rate and weight gain","Thyroid nodules or thyroid cancer can raise thyroid hormone secretion to abnormally high levels"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Iodine deficiency causes hypothyroidism and goitre.\n(2) Correct. Maternal hypothyroidism can cause cretinism, low IQ and deaf-mutism in the baby.\n(3) Incorrect. Exophthalmic goitre (Graves' disease) is a form of hyperthyroidism. It shows an increased BMR and weight loss, not a decreased BMR and weight gain.\n(4) Correct. Nodules or cancer of the thyroid lead to hyperthyroidism.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"The thymus gland is best described as a lobular structure located:","image":"","options":["Between the lungs, behind the sternum, on the ventral side of the aorta","On either side of the trachea, joined by an isthmus","Above each kidney","On the back side of the thyroid gland"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The thymus is a lobular gland that lies between the lungs, behind the sternum, on the ventral side of the aorta.\nThe two lobes on either side of the trachea, joined by an isthmus, describe the thyroid.\nThe glands above the kidneys are the adrenals.\nThe glands on the back of the thyroid are the parathyroids.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Thyroid hormones mostly regulate gene expression or chromosome function in their target cells.\nStatement II: Iodothyronines bind membrane-bound receptors and act mainly by generating cyclic AMP.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: iodothyronines, like steroids, interact with intracellular receptors. The hormone-receptor complex then interacts with the genome to regulate gene expression.\nStatement II is incorrect: generating second messengers such as cAMP is typical of hormones with membrane-bound receptors (for example, peptide hormones), not iodothyronines.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"An adult man has a pituitary disorder in which LH secretion is very low, while FSH secretion is normal. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["Normal spermatogenesis, because FSH alone regulates sperm formation","Increased muscle mass, because FSH takes over the anabolic role of androgens","Reduced testosterone output, impaired spermatogenesis and weakened function of accessory sex organs","Excess androgen secretion, because the Leydig cells are no longer under pituitary control"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In males, LH stimulates the Leydig cells to synthesise and secrete androgens. Low LH therefore means low testosterone.\nSpermatogenesis is regulated by FSH and androgens together, so normal FSH alone cannot keep it normal. This rules out option 1.\nLow androgens also weaken the accessory sex organs and reduce anabolic effects. This rules out options 2 and 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which hormone is secreted by the $\\alpha$-cells of the Islets of Langerhans?","image":"","options":["Insulin","Glucagon","Somatostatin","Secretin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The Islets of Langerhans have two main cell types, $\\alpha$-cells and $\\beta$-cells.\n$\\alpha$-cells secrete glucagon, and $\\beta$-cells secrete insulin.\nInsulin is the tempting distractor, but it is the $\\beta$-cell product.\nSecretin is a gastrointestinal hormone, and NCERT describes somatostatin as a hypothalamic hormone.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"During a neck surgery, all four parathyroid glands of a patient are accidentally removed, but the thyroid gland is left intact. Which of the following statements about the expected outcome is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Blood Ca²⁺ level would tend to fall","Reabsorption of Ca²⁺ by the renal tubules would decrease","The calcium-lowering action of thyrocalcitonin would remain unopposed","Demineralisation of bone would increase, releasing more Ca²⁺ into blood"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Removing the parathyroids removes PTH, the only hypercalcemic hormone described in the chapter.\n(1) Correct: without PTH, blood Ca²⁺ would tend to fall.\n(2) Correct: PTH-driven Ca²⁺ reabsorption in the renal tubules would decrease.\n(3) Correct: TCT comes from the intact thyroid, so its Ca²⁺-lowering action would act without opposition.\n(4) Incorrect: bone demineralisation is stimulated by PTH, so it would decrease, not increase.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The posterior pituitary synthesises oxytocin and vasopressin.\nStatement II: The posterior pituitary is under the direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Oxytocin and vasopressin are synthesised by the hypothalamus. They are carried along axons to the neurohypophysis, which only stores and releases them.\nStatement II is correct. The hypothalamus regulates the anterior pituitary through a portal circulation, but it controls the posterior pituitary directly through nerves.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which hormone is secreted by the pars intermedia of the pituitary gland?","image":"","options":["Prolactin","Adrenocorticotrophic hormone","Melanocyte stimulating hormone","Oxytocin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The pars intermedia secretes only one hormone, melanocyte stimulating hormone (MSH).\nMSH acts on melanocytes and regulates skin pigmentation.\nProlactin and ACTH come from the pars distalis.\nOxytocin is synthesised by the hypothalamus and released from the pars nervosa.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hormones that interact with membrane-bound receptors normally do not enter the target cell but generate second messengers such as cyclic AMP, IP3 and Ca++.\nStatement II: Iodothyronines interact with intracellular receptors, and the hormone-receptor complex mostly regulates gene expression by interacting with the genome.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Protein and peptide hormones bind membrane-bound receptors and act through second messengers (cAMP, IP3, Ca++), which regulate cellular metabolism.\nStatement II is correct. Steroid hormones and iodothyronines bind intracellular, mostly nuclear, receptors. Their hormone-receptor complex regulates gene expression or chromosome function.\nA common error is to think thyroid hormones act through membrane receptors because they are amino-acid based; NCERT groups them with steroids here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Leydig cells produce a group of hormones called","image":"","options":["Androgens","Estrogens","Aldosterone","Gonadotropins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Leydig cells (interstitial cells) lie in the spaces between the seminiferous tubules of the testis.\nThey secrete a group of hormones called androgens, mainly testosterone. Androgens control male accessory sex organs, secondary sexual characters and spermatogenesis.\nEstrogens are produced by the ovary.\nAldosterone comes from the adrenal cortex.\nGonadotropins (LH and FSH) come from the anterior pituitary. LH stimulates Leydig cells but is not produced by them.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones reach the anterior pituitary through a portal circulatory system.\nStatement II: Oxytocin and vasopressin are synthesised in the posterior pituitary and released under direct neural control of the hypothalamus.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Hypothalamic neurosecretory hormones are released from nerve endings. They then reach the anterior pituitary through the portal circulation and regulate its functions.\nStatement II is incorrect. The posterior pituitary is indeed under direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus. However, it only stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin. Both hormones are actually synthesised by the hypothalamus and transported axonally to the neurohypophysis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"After ovulation, the ruptured Graafian follicle changes into a structure that mainly secretes which hormone?","image":"","options":["Estrogen","Luteinizing hormone","Progesterone","Follicle stimulating hormone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Estrogen is secreted mainly by the growing ovarian follicles.\nAfter ovulation, the ruptured follicle becomes the corpus luteum. The corpus luteum secretes mainly progesterone, which supports pregnancy.\nLH (option 2) is tempting because it maintains the corpus luteum. However, LH is made by the anterior pituitary, not by the corpus luteum.\nFSH is also a pituitary hormone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are functions of the thyroid hormones $T_3$ and $T_4$?\n(i) Regulation of basal metabolic rate\n(ii) Support of red blood cell formation\n(iii) Control of carbohydrate, protein and fat metabolism\n(iv) Influence on water and electrolyte balance\n(v) Raising blood $Ca^{2+}$ by bone resorption\n(vi) Stimulating milk ejection from mammary glands","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $T_3$ and $T_4$ regulate the BMR.\n(ii) Correct: they support RBC formation.\n(iii) Correct: they control carbohydrate, protein and fat metabolism.\n(iv) Correct: they influence water and electrolyte balance.\n(v) Incorrect: bone resorption that raises blood $Ca^{2+}$ is the action of PTH. The thyroid's calcium hormone, TCT, actually lowers blood calcium.\n(vi) Incorrect: milk ejection is caused by oxytocin.\nSo four statements are functions of $T_3$ and $T_4$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of insulin?\n(i) Enhanced glucose uptake by adipocytes\n(ii) Glycogenesis in target cells\n(iii) Glycogenolysis in hepatocytes\n(iv) Stimulation of gluconeogenesis\n(v) Decrease in blood glucose level","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: insulin increases glucose uptake by adipocytes and hepatocytes.\n(ii) Yes: insulin stimulates the conversion of glucose to glycogen (glycogenesis).\n(iii) No: glycogenolysis is stimulated by glucagon.\n(iv) No: gluconeogenesis is also stimulated by glucagon.\n(v) Yes: the overall result of insulin action is hypoglycemia.\nSo three statements are effects of insulin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"The blood Ca²⁺ level of a person falls below normal. Which of the following correctly describes the expected hormonal response?","image":"","options":["Thyrocalcitonin secretion rises, which stimulates bone resorption","PTH secretion falls, so less Ca²⁺ is lost from the renal tubules","PTH secretion rises, which increases bone demineralisation, renal Ca²⁺ reabsorption and intestinal Ca²⁺ absorption","PTH secretion rises, which deposits Ca²⁺ in bone and increases urinary Ca²⁺ excretion"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"PTH secretion is regulated by circulating Ca²⁺, so a fall in Ca²⁺ calls for more PTH.\nPTH then raises blood Ca²⁺ in three ways: bone resorption, renal tubular reabsorption and absorption from food.\nOption (1) is wrong because TCT lowers blood Ca²⁺ and is not the hormone that causes bone resorption.\nOption (2) is wrong because PTH should rise, not fall.\nOption (4) is wrong because depositing Ca²⁺ in bone and losing it in urine would lower blood Ca²⁺ further.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A hormone binds to a receptor inside its target cell. The hormone-receptor complex then interacts with the genome and regulates gene expression. Which of the following hormones most likely acts in this way?","image":"","options":["Insulin","Glucagon","Cortisol","Epinephrine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT: steroid hormones and iodothyronines bind intracellular receptors, which are mostly nuclear receptors.\nThe hormone-receptor complex then interacts with the genome and regulates gene expression or chromosome function.\nCortisol is a steroid hormone, so it fits this mechanism.\nInsulin and glucagon are peptide hormones, and epinephrine is an amino-acid derivative. These bind membrane-bound receptors and act through second messengers. Epinephrine is the most tempting wrong choice, but it does not act on the genome through an intracellular receptor.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"According to the current scientific definition, hormones are:","image":"","options":["Nutrient chemicals stored in endocrine glands and released into blood","Non-nutrient chemicals that act as intercellular messengers and are produced in trace amounts","Enzymes secreted through ducts into target organs","Chemicals produced only by organised endocrine glands and carried by blood to distant organs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The current definition describes hormones as non-nutrient chemicals that act as intercellular messengers and are produced in trace amounts.\nOption (4) is the classical definition. It is narrower because it restricts hormones to organised endocrine glands and to blood transport to distant targets.\nOption (1) is wrong because hormones are non-nutrient.\nOption (3) is wrong because secretion through ducts describes exocrine glands.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about gonadal hormones are correct?\n(i) Testosterone and progesterone are steroid hormones that act through intracellular receptors.\n(ii) Androgens influence male sexual behaviour (libido) by acting on the central neural system.\n(iii) Estrogens are secreted mainly by the corpus luteum after ovulation.\n(iv) Androgens regulate the development and functions of accessory organs such as the seminal vesicles and prostate gland.\n(v) In males, LH stimulates the Leydig cells to secrete androgens, and FSH together with androgens regulates spermatogenesis.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT lists testosterone and progesterone as steroids. Steroids act through intracellular receptors and regulate gene expression.\n(ii) Correct: androgens act on the central neural system and influence libido.\n(iii) Incorrect: estrogen comes mainly from the growing ovarian follicles. The corpus luteum secretes mainly progesterone.\n(iv) Correct: androgens regulate the epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate gland and urethra.\n(v) Correct: LH stimulates androgen secretion from the testis, and FSH plus androgens regulate spermatogenesis.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Addison's disease, marked by acute weakness and fatigue due to altered carbohydrate metabolism, results from:","image":"","options":["Over-secretion of adrenaline by the adrenal medulla","Underproduction of hormones by the adrenal cortex","Excess secretion of growth hormone in adults","Deficiency of insulin from the β-cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that underproduction of adrenal cortex hormones alters carbohydrate metabolism.\nThis causes acute weakness and fatigue, a condition called Addison's disease.\nExcess adrenaline would cause stress-like effects, not this deficiency picture.\nExcess growth hormone in adults causes acromegaly.\nInsulin deficiency leads to diabetes mellitus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (action):\nList-I\n(a) Testosterone\n(b) Estrogen\n(c) Progesterone\n(d) LH (in females)\nList-II\n(i) Maintains the corpus luteum\n(ii) Low pitch of voice\n(iii) Supports pregnancy\n(iv) High pitch of voice","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Testosterone produces male secondary sex characters such as a low-pitched voice, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) Estrogen produces female secondary sex characters such as a high-pitched voice, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Progesterone supports pregnancy, so (c)-(iii).\n(d) In females, LH induces ovulation and maintains the corpus luteum, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Growth factors secreted by several non-endocrine tissues are needed for normal tissue growth and repair","Cholecystokinin acts on two different target organs","Atrial natriuretic factor is secreted mainly when blood pressure falls, and it raises blood pressure","Erythropoietin is a peptide hormone produced by a tissue that is not an organised endocrine gland"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: growth factors from non-endocrine tissues are essential for growth and for repair or regeneration.\n(2) Correct: CCK acts on both the pancreas and the gall bladder.\n(3) Incorrect: ANF is secreted when blood pressure rises, and it lowers blood pressure by vasodilation.\n(4) Correct: erythropoietin is a peptide hormone from the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following hormones are produced by the pars distalis of the pituitary?\nGH, MSH, oxytocin, FSH, ACTH, vasopressin, prolactin, LH","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The pars distalis (anterior pituitary) produces six hormones: GH, PRL, TSH, ACTH, LH and FSH.\nFrom the list, GH, FSH, ACTH, prolactin and LH belong to the pars distalis. That makes 5.\nMSH comes from the pars intermedia.\nOxytocin and vasopressin are released by the pars nervosa.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Parathyroid hormone and thyrocalcitonin are both secreted by the follicular cells of the thyroid gland.\nStatement II: Parathyroid hormone raises blood Ca²⁺ levels, whereas thyrocalcitonin lowers them.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. TCT comes from the thyroid gland, but PTH comes from the separate parathyroid glands. Also, the thyroid follicular cells make T3 and T4, not PTH.\nStatement II is correct. PTH is hypercalcemic, raising blood Ca²⁺, while TCT is hypocalcemic, lowering it.\nTogether, these two hormones maintain calcium balance in the body.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Invertebrates possess very simple endocrine systems with few hormones, whereas in vertebrates a large number of chemicals act as hormones.\nStatement II: The current definition of hormone is restricted to secretions of the organised endocrine glands only.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT contrasts the simple endocrine systems of invertebrates, with few hormones, against the many chemical messengers found in vertebrates.\nStatement II is incorrect: the new definition was adopted precisely because it covers many new molecules in addition to the hormones of organised endocrine glands.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"According to the chemical classification of hormones, which of the following is an amino-acid derivative?","image":"","options":["Cortisol","Insulin","Epinephrine","Estradiol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT groups hormones into four chemical classes: peptide/protein hormones, steroids, iodothyronines and amino-acid derivatives.\nEpinephrine (adrenaline) is the example NCERT gives of an amino-acid derivative.\nCortisol and estradiol are steroids.\nInsulin is a peptide hormone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Cretinism\n(B) Graves' disease\n(C) Thyrocalcitonin\n(D) Iodine-deficient diet\nList-II\n(i) Protein hormone that regulates blood calcium level\n(ii) Protrusion of eyeballs, increased BMR and weight loss\n(iii) Deaf-mutism and low IQ in a child due to maternal hypothyroidism\n(iv) Hypothyroidism with enlargement of thyroid (goitre)","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Hypothyroidism during pregnancy causes cretinism in the baby. Features include stunted growth, mental retardation, low IQ and deaf-mutism.\nB-(ii): Graves' disease (exophthalmic goitre) shows bulging eyes, high BMR and weight loss.\nC-(i): Thyrocalcitonin is a protein hormone that regulates blood $Ca^{2+}$.\nD-(iv): Lack of dietary iodine causes hypothyroidism and goitre.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): People whose diet is persistently deficient in iodine commonly develop an enlarged thyroid gland.\nReason (R): Iodine is required for the normal rate of synthesis of thyroid hormones.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Dietary iodine deficiency leads to hypothyroidism and enlargement of the thyroid, which is called goitre.\nR is true. Iodine is essential for normal synthesis of $T_3$ and $T_4$.\nR explains A. Without enough iodine, hormone synthesis falls, causing hypothyroidism along with enlargement of the gland.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (source):\nList-I\nA. Atrial natriuretic factor\nB. Erythropoietin\nC. Thymosins\nD. Melatonin\nList-II\n(i) Pineal gland\n(ii) Juxtaglomerular cells of kidney\n(iii) Atrial wall of heart\n(iv) Thymus","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): ANF is a peptide secreted by the atrial wall of the heart.\nB-(ii): Erythropoietin is produced by the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney.\nC-(iv): Thymosins are peptide hormones of the thymus.\nD-(i): Melatonin is secreted by the pineal gland.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Receptors for steroid hormones such as progesterone are mostly located in the:","image":"","options":["Cell membrane of the target cell","Nucleus of the target cell","Blood plasma","Cell wall of the target cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Steroid hormones bind intracellular receptors.\nNCERT notes that these intracellular receptors are mostly nuclear receptors.\nMembrane-bound receptors serve hormones that cannot enter the cell, such as peptide hormones.\nReceptors sit in target cells, not free in plasma, and animal cells have no cell wall.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Besides the neural system, the body needs a special kind of coordination and integration provided by hormones.\nReason (R): Nerve fibres do not innervate all cells of the body, while cellular functions need to be regulated continuously.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT says hormones provide a special kind of coordination in addition to neural coordination.\nR is true: nerve fibres do not reach every cell, and cellular functions need continuous regulation. Neural coordination is also short-lived.\nR is the very reason NCERT gives for why hormonal coordination is required, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A middle-aged adult develops gradual and severe disfigurement, especially of the face, due to excess secretion of a pituitary hormone. The disorder and the hormone responsible are:","image":"","options":["Gigantism - growth hormone","Acromegaly - growth hormone","Cretinism - thyroid stimulating hormone","Acromegaly - prolactin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Excess growth hormone in adults, especially in middle age, causes acromegaly. It produces severe disfigurement, mainly of the face.\nGigantism is also caused by GH over-secretion, but it occurs during the growth years and causes abnormal body growth.\nCretinism is linked to hypothyroidism during pregnancy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which cells of the thyroid gland synthesise tetraiodothyronine ($T_4$) and triiodothyronine ($T_3$)?","image":"","options":["Stromal cells lying between the follicles","Cells of the isthmus","Follicular cells enclosing a cavity","Chief cells present on the back of the thyroid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The thyroid is made of follicles and stromal tissue.\nEach follicle consists of follicular cells that enclose a cavity.\nThese follicular cells synthesise $T_4$ (thyroxine) and $T_3$.\nStromal tissue only supports the follicles.\nThe glands on the back of the thyroid are the parathyroids, and they secrete PTH, not thyroid hormones.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match the hormone in List-I with its chemical class in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Glucagon\n(B) Estradiol\n(C) Thyroxine ($T_4$)\n(D) Epinephrine\nList-II\n(I) Iodothyronine\n(II) Amino-acid derivative\n(III) Steroid\n(IV) Peptide/protein\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Glucagon is a peptide hormone secreted by the alpha-cells of the islets, so A-IV.\n(B) Estradiol is a steroid, like cortisol, testosterone and progesterone, so B-III.\n(C) Thyroxine is a thyroid hormone. NCERT places thyroid hormones in a separate class called iodothyronines, so C-I.\n(D) Epinephrine is NCERT's example of an amino-acid derivative, so D-II.\nOption (2) swaps C and D and is the tempting trap. NCERT lists iodothyronines and amino-acid derivatives as distinct classes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"In an experimental animal, the portal blood vessels linking the hypothalamus with the pituitary are blocked, but the nerve connections through the stalk remain intact. The release of which hormone would be LEAST affected?","image":"","options":["Luteinizing hormone","Growth hormone","Thyroid stimulating hormone","Vasopressin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones reach the anterior pituitary only through portal circulation.\nBlocking that circulation would disturb the regulation of LH, GH and TSH, which are all anterior pituitary hormones.\nVasopressin is made by hypothalamic neurons and travels down axons to the posterior pituitary, which is under direct neural control.\nSince the nerves are intact, vasopressin release would continue largely unaffected.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following hormones act mainly through intracellular receptors?\nCortisol, Insulin, Thyroxine, FSH, Progesterone, Glucagon, Testosterone, Epinephrine","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Steroids and iodothyronines use intracellular receptors.\nCortisol, progesterone and testosterone are steroids, so they count.\nThyroxine is an iodothyronine, so it counts.\nInsulin, FSH and glucagon are peptide/protein hormones and act through membrane-bound receptors.\nEpinephrine also acts through membrane-bound receptors.\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"On the basis of chemical nature, which of the following hormones is grouped by NCERT as an amino-acid derivative?","image":"","options":["Cortisol","Epinephrine","Glucagon","Estradiol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT groups hormones as peptide/protein, steroids, iodothyronines and amino-acid derivatives.\nEpinephrine is the example given for amino-acid derivatives.\nCortisol and estradiol are steroids.\nGlucagon is a peptide hormone.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are direct effects of parathyroid hormone?\n(i) Demineralisation of bone\n(ii) Reabsorption of Ca²⁺ by renal tubules\n(iii) Increased absorption of Ca²⁺ from digested food\n(iv) Increased loss of Ca²⁺ in urine\n(v) Lowering of blood Ca²⁺ level","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: PTH stimulates bone resorption.\n(ii) Correct: PTH stimulates Ca²⁺ reabsorption by the renal tubules.\n(iii) Correct: PTH increases Ca²⁺ absorption from digested food.\n(iv) Incorrect: increased tubular reabsorption reduces, rather than increases, Ca²⁺ loss in urine.\n(v) Incorrect: lowering blood Ca²⁺ is the action of TCT, while PTH raises it.\nSo three of the five are effects of PTH.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Acidic food from the stomach enters the duodenum. Two GI hormones are needed:\n(X) one that makes the exocrine pancreas release a watery, bicarbonate-rich fluid\n(Y) one that causes release of both bile juice and pancreatic enzymes.\nIdentify X and Y.","image":"","options":["X = Gastrin, Y = Secretin","X = Secretin, Y = Cholecystokinin","X = Cholecystokinin, Y = Gastric inhibitory peptide","X = Gastric inhibitory peptide, Y = Gastrin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"X releases water and bicarbonate ions from the exocrine pancreas. This is the action of secretin.\nY acts on both the gall bladder (bile juice) and the pancreas (enzymes). This is cholecystokinin.\nGastrin acts on gastric glands, not the pancreas. GIP inhibits gastric secretion and motility, so neither fits X or Y.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"The human pituitary gland lies in a bony cavity of the skull and is attached to the hypothalamus by a stalk. What is this cavity called?","image":"","options":["Foramen magnum","Sella turcica","Orbital fossa","Cranial ventricle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the pituitary gland sits in a bony cavity called the sella turcica.\nA stalk connects it to the hypothalamus.\nThe foramen magnum is the opening through which the spinal cord passes, so it is not the pituitary's seat.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Adrenaline and noradrenaline are called hormones of 'fight or flight'.\nReason (R): These catecholamines are rapidly secreted during stress and emergencies. They increase heart beat, the strength of heart contraction and the rate of respiration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the adrenal medulla hormones are called emergency or fight-or-flight hormones.\nR is true: they are released quickly during stress of any kind. They raise heart rate, the force of heart contraction and the breathing rate. They also cause alertness, pupillary dilation and sweating.\nThese rapid responses prepare the body to face or escape danger. R therefore explains why A is the name given.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Neural coordination, as compared to hormonal coordination, is best described as:","image":"","options":["Slow and long-lasting","Slow and short-lived","Fast but short-lived","Fast and reaching every cell of the body"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes neural coordination as point-to-point and rapid, that is, fast but short-lived.\nOption (4) is wrong because nerve fibres do not innervate all cells of the body. This limitation is exactly why hormonal coordination is also needed.\nOptions (1) and (2) are wrong because neural coordination is fast, not slow.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Thymosin is responsible for","image":"","options":["Raising the blood sugar level","Raising the blood calcium level","Differentiation of T lymphocytes","Decrease in blood RBC"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The thymus secretes peptide hormones called thymosins.\nThymosins play a major role in the differentiation of T-lymphocytes, which provide cell-mediated immunity. They also promote antibody production, which supports humoral immunity.\nRaising blood calcium is the job of parathyroid hormone (PTH).\nRaising blood sugar is done by glucagon and cortisol.\nThymosins do not decrease the RBC count.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Hypothalamus\n(B) Sella tursica\n(C) Hypophyseal portal system\n(D) Neurohypophysis\nList-II\n(i) Stores and releases hormones made by hypothalamic neurons\n(ii) Basal diencephalon containing neurosecretory nuclei\n(iii) Carries releasing and inhibiting hormones to the anterior pituitary\n(iv) Bony cavity lodging the pituitary gland","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-i, B-iv, C-iii, D-ii","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-ii: the hypothalamus is the basal diencephalon and contains neurosecretory nuclei.\nB-iv: the pituitary sits in a bony cavity called the sella tursica.\nC-iii: the portal circulation carries hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones to the anterior pituitary.\nD-i: the neurohypophysis stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin, which are made by hypothalamic neurons.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Cells in the atrial wall of the heart, which is not an organised endocrine gland, secrete a non-nutrient peptide in trace amounts that acts as a messenger on other cells. Which statement about this peptide is correct?","image":"","options":["It is not a hormone, because only ductless glands produce hormones","It is a hormone under the classical definition but not under the current definition","It qualifies as a hormone under the current definition, which covers molecules beyond those of organised endocrine glands","It is an enzyme, because it is produced by a non-glandular tissue"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The current definition calls hormones non-nutrient intercellular messengers produced in trace amounts. It does not require an organised endocrine gland as the source.\nNCERT notes that the new definition covers many new molecules. It also states that the heart produces hormones, for example atrial natriuretic factor (ANF).\nOption (2) reverses the logic: the classical definition demands production by endocrine glands, so it is the narrower one.\nOption (1) wrongly limits hormones to ductless glands, and option (4) wrongly calls the peptide an enzyme.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Hypothalamic hormones are released from the nerve endings of hypothalamic neurons.\n(ii) All hormones of the hypothalamus stimulate the secretion of pituitary hormones.\n(iii) Somatostatin is produced by the anterior pituitary itself.\n(iv) GnRH acts on the anterior pituitary.\n(v) The posterior pituitary receives hypothalamic control mainly through portal blood.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: these hormones pass through axons and are released at the nerve endings.\n(ii) Incorrect: the hypothalamus also makes inhibiting hormones.\n(iii) Incorrect: NCERT places somatostatin's origin in the hypothalamus.\n(iv) Correct: gonadotrophins come from the pars distalis, the anterior pituitary.\n(v) Incorrect: the posterior pituitary is under direct neural control.\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A hormone released into the blood reaches almost every tissue, yet it acts only on certain tissues. The best explanation is that:","image":"","options":["The hormone is destroyed in all non-target tissues before reaching them","Specific hormone receptors are present only in the target tissues","Only target tissues receive blood supply","The hormone enters every cell but is active only in the nucleus of target cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hormones act by binding to specific proteins called hormone receptors.\nThese receptors are located in the target tissues only.\nTissues that lack the receptor cannot form a hormone-receptor complex, so they show no response.\nAll tissues have a blood supply, so option (3) is wrong.\nPeptide hormones do not even enter cells, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Hormones are called chemical signals that stimulate specific target tissues. Their action depends on the presence of receptors on the respective target tissues. Which of the following is the correct location of the receptors in the case of protein hormones?","image":"","options":["Extra cellular matrix","Blood","Plasma membrane","Nucleus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: protein and peptide hormones are large and water-soluble, so they cannot cross the lipid bilayer.\nThey therefore bind to receptors on the plasma membrane of the target cell. This triggers second messengers such as cAMP and $Ca^{2+}$, which carry the signal inside the cell.\nNuclear (intracellular) receptors are used by lipid-soluble steroid and iodothyronine hormones, so 'nucleus' is the tempting wrong option.\nReceptors are never located in the blood or the extracellular matrix.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q11)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Parathyroid hormone (PTH) belongs to which chemical class of hormones?","image":"","options":["Steroid","Iodothyronine","Amino-acid derivative","Peptide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT describes PTH as a peptide hormone secreted by the parathyroid glands.\nSteroid hormones include cortisol, testosterone, estradiol and progesterone.\nIodothyronines are the thyroid hormones T3 and T4. They are produced by the nearby thyroid gland, which makes them a tempting but wrong choice.\nEpinephrine is an example of an amino-acid derivative.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"The groups of hormone-producing neurosecretory cells present in the hypothalamus are called","image":"","options":["ganglia","nuclei","islets","follicles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The hypothalamus contains several groups of neurosecretory cells called nuclei, and these nuclei produce hormones.\nGanglia are clusters of nerve cell bodies outside the central nervous system.\nIslets (of Langerhans) are found in the pancreas.\nFollicles occur in the thyroid and the ovary.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which layer forms the innermost part of the human adrenal cortex?","image":"","options":["Zona glomerulosa","Zona fasciculata","Zona reticularis","Adrenal medulla"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The adrenal cortex has three layers. From outside inwards they are zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata and zona reticularis.\nSo zona reticularis is the inner layer of the cortex.\nThe adrenal medulla lies at the centre of the gland, inside the cortex. It is a separate tissue and not a layer of the cortex, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Mary is about to face an interview. But during the first five minutes before the interview she experiences sweating, increased rate of heart beat, respiration etc. Which of the following hormones are responsible for her restlessness?","image":"","options":["Estrogen and progesterone","Oxytocin and vasopressin","Adrenaline and noradrenaline","Insulin and glucagon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mary's signs are typical of stress: sweating, faster heartbeat and faster breathing.\nIn stress or emergency, the adrenal medulla rapidly releases the catecholamines adrenaline and noradrenaline. These are called the 'fight or flight' hormones.\nThey raise heart rate, strength of heart contraction, rate of respiration, sweating, alertness and blood glucose.\nInsulin and glucagon mainly control blood sugar, and oxytocin and vasopressin act on the uterus and kidney. Neither pair explains these acute stress signs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"In the Islets of Langerhans of the human pancreas, which cells secrete the hyperglycemic hormone glucagon?","image":"","options":["$\\beta$-cells","$\\alpha$-cells","Juxtaglomerular cells","Leydig cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The two main cell types of the Islets of Langerhans are $\\alpha$-cells and $\\beta$-cells.\n$\\alpha$-cells secrete glucagon, which raises blood sugar.\n$\\beta$-cells secrete insulin, which lowers blood sugar. This option is tempting only if the two cell types are mixed up.\nJuxtaglomerular cells of the kidney make erythropoietin. Leydig cells of the testis make androgens.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glucagon is described as a hyperglycemic hormone.\nReason (R): Glucagon acts on hepatocytes and stimulates the conversion of glucose into glycogen.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Glucagon raises blood glucose by two routes:\n- it stimulates glycogenolysis (breakdown of glycogen) in hepatocytes;\n- it stimulates gluconeogenesis.\nIt also reduces cellular glucose uptake and utilisation.\nR is false. Converting glucose into glycogen is glycogenesis, which is stimulated by insulin, not glucagon.\nGlycogenesis would lower blood sugar, so it cannot explain hyperglycemia.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are functions of the thyroid hormones $T_3$ and $T_4$?\n(i) Regulation of basal metabolic rate\n(ii) Supporting red blood cell formation\n(iii) Control of fat metabolism\n(iv) Influencing water and electrolyte balance\n(v) Raising blood $Ca^{2+}$ level by bone resorption\n(vi) Regulating the 24-hour sleep-wake rhythm","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: thyroid hormones regulate BMR.\n(ii) Correct: they support RBC formation.\n(iii) Correct: they control the metabolism of carbohydrates, proteins and fats.\n(iv) Correct: they influence water and electrolyte balance.\n(v) Incorrect: raising blood $Ca^{2+}$ by bone resorption is done by PTH; thyrocalcitonin actually lowers blood calcium.\n(vi) Incorrect: the sleep-wake rhythm is regulated by melatonin from the pineal gland.\nTotal correct = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Cortisol is secreted by","image":"","options":["Pancreas","Thyroid","Adrenal","Thymus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: the adrenal cortex has three zones. The zona fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids, and cortisol is the main one.\nCortisol raises blood glucose through gluconeogenesis and suppresses the immune response.\nThe pancreas secretes insulin and glucagon, not cortisol. The thyroid secretes $T_3$, $T_4$ and calcitonin. The thymus secretes thymosins.\nSo cortisol comes from the adrenal gland.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Gastrin, a peptide hormone of the gastrointestinal tract, acts on gastric glands and stimulates the secretion of","image":"","options":["Bile juice and pancreatic enzymes","Water and bicarbonate ions","Hydrochloric acid and pepsinogen","Insulin and glucagon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Gastrin targets the gastric glands and stimulates them to secrete hydrochloric acid and pepsinogen.\nWater and bicarbonate secretion from the exocrine pancreas is the action of secretin.\nBile and pancreatic enzyme secretion is the action of CCK.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"In an experimental animal, thyrocalcitonin is secreted in excess for some time. Which change is most likely to occur as a result?","image":"","options":["Blood $Ca^{2+}$ rises, and PTH secretion falls","Blood $Ca^{2+}$ falls, and PTH secretion rises to promote bone resorption","Blood $Ca^{2+}$ falls, and PTH secretion also falls","Blood $Ca^{2+}$ is unchanged, because TCT only affects BMR"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"TCT, a protein hormone from the thyroid, lowers blood calcium.\nPTH secretion is regulated by circulating $Ca^{2+}$ levels, so falling calcium stimulates more PTH release.\nPTH is hypercalcemic. It promotes bone resorption, renal $Ca^{2+}$ reabsorption and intestinal $Ca^{2+}$ absorption.\nTCT and PTH therefore work together to maintain calcium balance.\nOption (3) fails because low calcium would raise, not lower, PTH secretion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A person has a severe deficiency of aldosterone while all other adrenal hormones are normal. Which set of changes is most expected?","image":"","options":["Increased Na$^+$ retention, increased K$^+$ excretion, rise in blood pressure","Increased loss of Na$^+$ and water in urine, retention of K$^+$, fall in blood volume and blood pressure","Rise in blood glucose due to enhanced gluconeogenesis and suppressed immunity","Increased RBC production and anti-inflammatory response"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aldosterone normally promotes renal reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water and excretion of K$^+$ and phosphate.\nWithout it, Na$^+$ and water are lost in urine while K$^+$ is retained.\nThis lowers body fluid volume and therefore blood pressure.\nOption 1 describes aldosterone excess, the opposite situation.\nOptions 3 and 4 describe glucocorticoid (cortisol) actions, which are normal here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Hormone)\n(a) Parathyroid hormone\n(b) Thyrocalcitonin\n(c) Aldosterone\n(d) Thyroxine\nList-II (Feature)\n(i) Protein hormone that lowers blood calcium\n(ii) Peptide hormone that raises blood calcium\n(iii) Regulates basal metabolic rate\n(iv) Stimulates Na⁺ reabsorption in renal tubules","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): PTH is a peptide hormone and is hypercalcemic.\n(b)-(i): TCT is a protein hormone from the thyroid that lowers blood Ca²⁺.\n(c)-(iv): aldosterone stimulates Na⁺ and water reabsorption at the renal tubules.\n(d)-(iii): thyroid hormones such as thyroxine regulate the basal metabolic rate.\nOption (1) swaps the PTH and TCT pairs, which is the most tempting error.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glucagon is called a hyperglycemic hormone.\nReason (R): Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis in hepatocytes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: glucagon raises the blood sugar level, so it is a hyperglycemic hormone.\nR is true: glucagon acts on hepatocytes and stimulates the breakdown of glycogen (glycogenolysis) and gluconeogenesis.\nBoth processes release glucose into the blood, which causes hyperglycemia.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Somatostatin secreted by the hypothalamus directly inhibits the release of which pituitary hormone?","image":"","options":["Prolactin","Adrenocorticotrophic hormone","Growth hormone","Vasopressin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Somatostatin is NCERT's example of a hypothalamic inhibiting hormone.\nIt inhibits the release of growth hormone (GH) from the pituitary.\nProlactin and ACTH are other anterior pituitary hormones, but they are not the example linked to somatostatin.\nVasopressin is released by the posterior pituitary, which is under direct neural control.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher treats target cells with a drug that blocks transcription (synthesis of mRNA from DNA) but does not affect existing enzymes or membrane signalling. The early response to which hormone is most likely to be directly blocked?","image":"","options":["Glucagon acting on hepatocytes","Epinephrine acting on heart cells","Progesterone acting on uterine cells","A hypothalamic releasing hormone acting on pituitary cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Progesterone is a steroid hormone. It binds intracellular (mostly nuclear) receptors, and the complex acts on the genome to regulate gene expression. Blocking transcription therefore blocks its response.\nGlucagon, epinephrine and hypothalamic hormones bind membrane-bound receptors. Their second messengers modify existing cellular machinery quickly, so their early responses do not depend on new transcription.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (condition) with List-II (hormonal cause):\nList-I\n(A) Gigantism\n(B) Pituitary dwarfism\n(C) Acromegaly\n(D) Diabetes insipidus\nList-II\n(i) Excess GH in adults, especially middle age\n(ii) Impaired synthesis or release of ADH\n(iii) Over-secretion of GH during the growing years\n(iv) Low secretion of GH\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Gigantism: over-secretion of GH causes abnormal growth of the body, so A-(iii).\n(B) Pituitary dwarfism: low GH secretion causes stunted growth, so B-(iv).\n(C) Acromegaly: excess GH in middle-aged adults causes severe disfigurement, especially of the face, so C-(i).\n(D) Diabetes insipidus: impaired ADH leads to water loss, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Insulin normally does not enter its target cell to bring about its effects.\nReason (R): Insulin binds to membrane-bound receptors and its action is carried inside the cell by second messengers.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Insulin is a peptide hormone, and such hormones usually stay outside the target cell.\nR is true. Peptide hormones bind membrane-bound receptors and generate second messengers such as cAMP, IP3 and Ca++. These second messengers regulate cell metabolism.\nR explains why the hormone does not need to enter the cell.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Select the right match of endocrine gland and their hormone among the options given below\nA. Pineal — i. Epinephrine\nB. Thyroid — ii. Melatonin\nC. Ovary — iii. Estrogen\nD. Adrenal medulla — iv. Tetraiodothyronine","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Pineal gland: secretes melatonin, so A–ii.\nThyroid: secretes tetraiodothyronine ($T_4$, thyroxine), so B–iv.\nOvary: the growing follicles secrete estrogen, so C–iii.\nAdrenal medulla: secretes epinephrine (adrenaline), so D–i.\nOnly option (4) has A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i. Option (2) also gets A and B right but swaps C and D, and the ovary does not make epinephrine.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Adrenaline and noradrenaline are collectively called catecholamines. Which part of the body secretes them?","image":"","options":["Adrenal cortex","Adrenal medulla","Posterior pituitary","Zona glomerulosa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the centrally located adrenal medulla secretes adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline (norepinephrine). Together these are called catecholamines.\nThe adrenal cortex, including its outer zona glomerulosa, secretes corticoids, not catecholamines.\nThe posterior pituitary releases oxytocin and vasopressin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram shows the dorsal view of the thyroid gland. It has two lobes on either side of the trachea, and small oval glands are embedded on the back of the lobes. How many of these small glands are there, and how are they arranged?","image":"","options":["Two glands, one in each lobe","Four glands, one pair in each lobe","Four glands, all in the isthmus","Six glands, three in each lobe"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that humans have four parathyroid glands on the back side of the thyroid gland.\nThey are arranged as one pair in each of the two thyroid lobes, as shown in Figure 19.3(b).\nThe isthmus is only a thin connective tissue flap joining the two lobes, so it does not hold these glands.\n'Two glands' is wrong because each lobe carries a pair, not a single gland.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"The steroid responsible for balance of water and electrolytes in our body is","image":"","options":["Insulin","Melatonin","Testosterone","Aldosterone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Aldosterone is the main mineralocorticoid, a steroid secreted by the adrenal cortex.\nIn the renal tubules it increases reabsorption of $Na^+$ and water and increases excretion of $K^+$ and phosphate. This maintains electrolyte and body-fluid balance and blood pressure.\nTestosterone is also a steroid, but it is a male sex hormone and does not regulate water and electrolytes.\nInsulin is a peptide hormone and melatonin is an amine, so neither is a steroid.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following hormones tend to raise blood glucose by stimulating glycogenolysis and/or gluconeogenesis?\nGlucagon, Insulin, Cortisol, Adrenaline, Aldosterone","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Glucagon: yes. It stimulates glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis in hepatocytes.\nInsulin: no. It increases glucose uptake and glycogenesis, so blood glucose falls.\nCortisol: yes. As a glucocorticoid, it stimulates gluconeogenesis.\nAdrenaline: yes. It stimulates glycogen breakdown, which raises blood glucose.\nAldosterone: no. It regulates Na+, K+ and water balance.\nThe total is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Somatostatin, a hormone produced by the hypothalamus, directly affects the pituitary by:","image":"","options":["Stimulating the release of gonadotrophins from the anterior pituitary","Inhibiting the release of growth hormone from the pituitary","Stimulating the release of oxytocin from the posterior pituitary","Inhibiting the synthesis of thyroxine in the thyroid follicles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hypothalamic hormones are of two types. Releasing hormones stimulate pituitary secretion. Inhibiting hormones suppress it.\nNCERT gives somatostatin as the example of an inhibiting hormone. It inhibits the release of growth hormone (GH) from the pituitary.\nOption (1) describes GnRH, which is a releasing hormone, not somatostatin.\nOption (4) is wrong because somatostatin acts on the pituitary in this context, not on the thyroid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT listed by NCERT as an organised endocrine body in humans?","image":"","options":["Thymus","Pineal","Kidney","Parathyroid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the organised endocrine bodies as pituitary, pineal, thyroid, adrenal, pancreas, parathyroid, thymus and gonads.\nThe kidney does produce hormones. However, NCERT places it with the gastrointestinal tract, liver and heart, as an organ that is not an organised endocrine gland.\nThymus, pineal and parathyroid are all organised endocrine bodies.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are organised endocrine bodies (not merely hormone-producing organs) in the human body?\nPineal, Liver, Thymus, Heart, Parathyroid, Kidney, Gonads, Gastrointestinal tract","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Organised endocrine bodies in this list: pineal, thymus, parathyroid and gonads. That makes 4.\nThe liver, heart, kidney and gastrointestinal tract also produce hormones. However, NCERT lists them separately, as organs other than the organised endocrine glands.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Thymosins play a major role in the differentiation of T-lymphocytes, which provide cell-mediated immunity.\nStatement II: Thymosins have no role in humoral immunity.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: thymosins drive the differentiation of T-lymphocytes, which provide cell-mediated immunity.\nStatement II is incorrect: thymosins also promote the production of antibodies, which provide humoral immunity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Why is the pancreas described as a composite gland?","image":"","options":["It is made of both nervous and glandular tissue","It acts as both an exocrine and an endocrine gland","It secretes both steroid and peptide hormones","It develops from both ectoderm and endoderm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT calls the pancreas a composite gland because it works as both an exocrine gland and an endocrine gland.\nThe endocrine part consists of the Islets of Langerhans.\nThe islet hormones, insulin and glucagon, are both peptides, so option (3) is wrong.\nOption (1) describes the pituitary-hypothalamus link, not the pancreas.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A person's adrenal cortex secretes abnormally large amounts of aldosterone over a long period. Which combination of changes is most expected?","image":"","options":["Increased plasma K$^+$, decreased body fluid volume, low blood pressure","Increased Na$^+$ and water retention, decreased plasma K$^+$, raised blood pressure","Decreased Na$^+$ reabsorption, increased phosphate retention, raised blood pressure","Increased loss of water in urine, increased plasma K$^+$, low osmotic pressure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aldosterone acts on the renal tubules. It increases reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water and increases excretion of K$^+$ and phosphate.\nExcess aldosterone therefore causes Na$^+$ and water retention and a fall in plasma K$^+$.\nThe retained water increases body fluid volume, which raises blood pressure.\nThe other options either reverse the K$^+$ change or describe water loss, which is the opposite effect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which hormone is secreted by the atrial wall of the human heart when blood pressure rises?","image":"","options":["Erythropoietin","Atrial natriuretic factor","Aldosterone","Vasopressin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The atrial wall of the heart secretes a peptide hormone called atrial natriuretic factor (ANF).\nANF is released when blood pressure increases. It dilates blood vessels and so lowers the pressure.\nErythropoietin comes from the kidney. Aldosterone comes from the adrenal cortex. Vasopressin is released from the posterior pituitary.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Oxytocin and vasopressin are described as hormones of the posterior pituitary.\nReason (R): Oxytocin and vasopressin are synthesised by the cells of the neurohypophysis itself.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: the neurohypophysis (pars nervosa) stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin.\nR is false: these hormones are actually synthesised in the hypothalamus.\nThey travel along axons to the neurohypophysis, where they are stored.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Insulin acts mainly on which of the following cells to enhance cellular glucose uptake?","image":"","options":["Hepatocytes and adipocytes","Erythrocytes and neurons","Osteocytes and chondrocytes","Melanocytes and keratinocytes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Insulin acts mainly on hepatocytes (liver cells) and adipocytes (cells of adipose tissue).\nIn these cells it enhances glucose uptake and utilisation.\nGlucose therefore moves rapidly from the blood into these cells, and the blood glucose level falls.\nThe other cell pairs are not described in NCERT as the main targets of insulin.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"The pituitary gland is lodged in a bony cavity and connected to the hypothalamus by a stalk. What is this bony cavity called?","image":"","options":["Foramen magnum","Sella turcica","Cranial fossa of the occipital bone","Orbit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The pituitary sits in a depression of bone called the sella turcica.\nA stalk attaches it to the hypothalamus.\nThe foramen magnum is the opening through which the spinal cord leaves the skull, so it cannot house the pituitary.\nThe orbit holds the eyeball.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $T_3$ and $T_4$ act through intracellular receptors and mostly regulate gene expression, whereas thyrocalcitonin, being a protein hormone, acts through membrane-bound receptors and second messengers.\nStatement II: An enlarged thyroid gland always indicates that the gland is under-secreting its hormones.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Iodothyronines ($T_3$, $T_4$) bind intracellular receptors, and the hormone-receptor complex regulates gene expression. Protein hormones like TCT bind membrane-bound receptors and generate second messengers such as cAMP.\nStatement II is incorrect. Goitre from iodine deficiency is hypothyroid, but exophthalmic goitre is also an enlarged thyroid with hyperthyroidism.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about calcium regulation is incorrect?","image":"","options":["PTH stimulates resorption (demineralisation) of bone","PTH decreases Ca$^{2+}$ absorption from digested food","Thyrocalcitonin is a protein hormone secreted by the thyroid gland","Secretion of PTH is regulated by the circulating levels of calcium ions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: PTH acts on bone and promotes its dissolution.\n(2) Incorrect: PTH increases Ca$^{2+}$ absorption from digested food. It also increases renal reabsorption of Ca$^{2+}$, which makes it a hypercalcemic hormone.\n(3) Correct: TCT is a protein hormone of the thyroid that regulates blood calcium.\n(4) Correct: Circulating Ca$^{2+}$ levels control how much PTH is secreted.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Shortly after a person eats a carbohydrate-rich meal, the blood glucose level rises. Which combination correctly describes the main hormonal response that brings the level back to normal?","image":"","options":["$\\alpha$-cells release glucagon, which stimulates glycogenolysis in hepatocytes","$\\beta$-cells release insulin, which promotes glucose entry into hepatocytes and adipocytes and stimulates glycogenesis","$\\beta$-cells release glucagon, which reduces cellular glucose uptake","$\\alpha$-cells release insulin, which stimulates gluconeogenesis in the liver"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A rise in blood glucose has to be corrected by a hypoglycemic hormone, which is insulin from $\\beta$-cells.\nInsulin moves glucose into hepatocytes and adipocytes and promotes glycogenesis, so blood glucose falls.\nOption (1) is a real pathway, but glucagon raises blood glucose, which would make the situation worse.\nOptions (3) and (4) pair each hormone with the wrong cell type, and option (4) also gives insulin the wrong action.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Melatonin\nB. Thymosins\nC. Thymus\nD. T-lymphocytes\nList-II:\n(i) Provide cell-mediated immunity\n(ii) Lobular structure between the lungs behind the sternum\n(iii) Maintains the sleep-wake rhythm\n(iv) Peptide hormones aiding T-lymphocyte differentiation","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-iii: melatonin from the pineal gland maintains the sleep-wake rhythm.\nB-iv: thymosins are peptide hormones that aid T-lymphocyte differentiation.\nC-ii: the thymus is a lobular structure between the lungs, behind the sternum.\nD-i: T-lymphocytes provide cell-mediated immunity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A tumour in the outermost layer (zona glomerulosa) of the adrenal cortex causes excessive secretion of its main hormone. Which combination of changes is most likely to be observed?","image":"","options":["Increased renal reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water, increased K$^+$ excretion, and a rise in blood pressure","Decreased renal reabsorption of Na$^+$, decreased K$^+$ excretion, and a fall in blood pressure","Increased renal reabsorption of Na$^+$, decreased K$^+$ excretion, and a fall in blood volume","Increased gluconeogenesis, suppressed immune response, and decreased RBC production"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The zona glomerulosa secretes mineralocorticoids, mainly aldosterone.\nAldosterone acts on the renal tubules. It increases reabsorption of Na+ and water and increases excretion of K+ and phosphate.\nRetaining Na+ and water expands body fluid volume, so blood pressure rises. This matches option (1).\nOption (4) describes glucocorticoid (cortisol) effects. It is also wrong in detail, because cortisol stimulates RBC production rather than decreasing it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Secretin acts on the exocrine pancreas and stimulates secretion of water and bicarbonate ions.\nStatement II: Cholecystokinin acts only on the gall bladder and has no effect on the pancreas.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Secretin targets the exocrine pancreas and causes release of water and bicarbonate ions.\nStatement II is incorrect. CCK acts on both the pancreas and the gall bladder.\nIt stimulates secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice respectively.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the mechanism of hormone action is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Each hormone receptor is specific to one hormone only","Hormones that bind membrane-bound receptors usually do not enter the cell and act through second messengers such as cAMP, IP$_3$ and Ca$^{2+}$","Iodothyronines act through membrane-bound receptors and generate cyclic AMP as the second messenger","Steroid hormone–receptor complexes mostly regulate gene expression by interacting with the genome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: receptors are specific, and each binds only one hormone.\n(2) Correct: protein and peptide hormones bind surface receptors and generate second messengers.\n(3) Incorrect: iodothyronines (thyroid hormones), like steroids, bind intracellular receptors. They regulate gene expression rather than generating cAMP.\n(4) Correct: steroid hormone–receptor complexes interact with the genome.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A person lives for a long time on a diet severely deficient in iodine. Which condition is this person most likely to develop?","image":"","options":["Exophthalmic goitre with increased basal metabolic rate","Acromegaly","Hypothyroidism with enlargement of the thyroid (goitre)","Diabetes insipidus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Iodine is essential for normal synthesis of thyroid hormones.\nDietary iodine deficiency causes hypothyroidism and enlargement of the thyroid, commonly called goitre.\nExophthalmic goitre is a form of hyperthyroidism, which is the opposite hormonal state.\nAcromegaly is caused by excess growth hormone in adults.\nDiabetes insipidus is caused by impaired ADH synthesis or release.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (GI tract hormone) with List-II (action):\nList-I\nA. Gastrin\nB. Secretin\nC. Cholecystokinin\nD. Gastric inhibitory peptide\nList-II\n(i) Secretion of water and bicarbonate ions from exocrine pancreas\n(ii) Inhibits gastric secretion and motility\n(iii) Secretion of HCl and pepsinogen by gastric glands\n(iv) Secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Gastrin acts on gastric glands and triggers release of HCl and pepsinogen.\nB-(i): Secretin acts on the exocrine pancreas and increases secretion of water and bicarbonate.\nC-(iv): CCK acts on both the pancreas and the gall bladder, releasing pancreatic enzymes and bile.\nD-(ii): GIP inhibits gastric secretion and motility.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly pairs a hormone produced by a non-endocrine organ with its source and action?","image":"","options":["Erythropoietin – juxtaglomerular cells of kidney – stimulates formation of RBCs","Atrial natriuretic factor – ventricular wall of heart – raises blood pressure","Erythropoietin – atrial wall of heart – stimulates formation of RBCs","Atrial natriuretic factor – juxtaglomerular cells of kidney – constricts blood vessels"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: The juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney secrete the peptide hormone erythropoietin, which stimulates erythropoiesis (RBC formation).\nANF comes from the atrial wall of the heart, not the ventricle or the kidney.\nWhen blood pressure rises, ANF dilates blood vessels and so lowers blood pressure. Options (2) and (4) therefore get both its source and its action wrong.\nOption (3) gives erythropoietin the wrong source, the atrial wall.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Adrenaline and noradrenaline, commonly called catecholamines, are secreted by the:","image":"","options":["Adrenal cortex","Adrenal medulla","Posterior pituitary","Thyroid follicles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The centrally located adrenal medulla secretes adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline (norepinephrine).\nTogether these are called catecholamines.\nThe adrenal cortex secretes corticoids such as cortisol and aldosterone, not catecholamines.\nThe posterior pituitary releases oxytocin and vasopressin, and thyroid follicles make T3 and T4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of adrenaline and noradrenaline during an emergency?\nIncreased alertness; pupillary constriction; piloerection; sweating; decreased heart rate; glycogenolysis; lipolysis","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Correct effects: increased alertness, piloerection, sweating, glycogenolysis and lipolysis.\nCatecholamines stimulate the breakdown of glycogen, lipids and proteins.\nPupillary constriction is wrong, because these hormones cause pupillary dilation.\nDecreased heart rate is wrong, because they increase both the heart beat and the strength of contraction.\nSo the number of correct effects is 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT an effect of the 'fight or flight' hormones secreted by the adrenal medulla?","image":"","options":["Piloerection","Increased sweating","Constriction of the pupils","Increased rate of respiration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Adrenaline and noradrenaline prepare the body for emergencies.\nTheir listed effects include increased alertness, pupillary dilation, piloerection (raising of hairs) and sweating. They also raise the heart beat, the strength of heart contraction and the rate of respiration.\nThe pupils dilate, not constrict, so option (3) is not an effect of these hormones.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are actions of glucocorticoids as described in NCERT?\nGluconeogenesis; lipolysis; proteolysis; stimulation of cellular uptake of amino acids; anti-inflammatory reactions; enhancement of the immune response; stimulation of RBC production","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Gluconeogenesis, lipolysis and proteolysis are all correct. Glucocorticoids stimulate each of these.\nUptake of amino acids is incorrect. Glucocorticoids inhibit the cellular uptake and utilisation of amino acids.\nAnti-inflammatory reactions are correct. Cortisol produces these.\nEnhancement of immunity is incorrect. Cortisol suppresses the immune response.\nRBC production is correct. Cortisol stimulates it.\nThat gives 5 correct actions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An impairment in the synthesis or release of ADH leads to water loss and dehydration.\nReason (R): ADH stimulates resorption of water and electrolytes by the distal tubules of the kidney.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. When ADH is deficient, the kidney cannot conserve water. This causes water loss and dehydration, a condition called diabetes insipidus.\nR is true. Vasopressin (ADH) acts on the distal tubules and stimulates resorption of water and electrolytes, which reduces diuresis.\nWithout ADH, this resorption fails, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following hormones tend to raise the blood glucose level?\nGlucagon, Insulin, Adrenaline, Cortisol, Atrial natriuretic factor, Thyrocalcitonin","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Glucagon raises blood glucose. It stimulates glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis in hepatocytes.\nAdrenaline raises blood glucose. It stimulates glycogen breakdown.\nCortisol raises blood glucose. It stimulates gluconeogenesis.\nInsulin lowers blood glucose (hypoglycemic).\nANF lowers blood pressure, and thyrocalcitonin regulates blood calcium; neither affects glucose.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (adrenal hormone) with List-II (action):\nList-I\n(a) Cortisol\n(b) Aldosterone\n(c) Adrenaline\n(d) Adrenal androgens\nList-II\n(i) Growth of axial, pubic and facial hair during puberty\n(ii) Suppresses the immune response and produces anti-inflammatory reactions\n(iii) Reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water from renal tubules\n(iv) Pupillary dilation and piloerection","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Cortisol, the main glucocorticoid, is anti-inflammatory and suppresses the immune response, so a matches ii.\n(b) Aldosterone stimulates Na$^+$ and water reabsorption in the renal tubules, so b matches iii.\n(c) Adrenaline, a fight-or-flight hormone, causes pupillary dilation and piloerection, so c matches iv.\n(d) Small amounts of adrenal androgens support growth of axial, pubic and facial hair at puberty, so d matches i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"The hypothalamus, which regulates a wide range of body functions, is anatomically the","image":"","options":["dorsal part of the midbrain","basal part of the diencephalon of the forebrain","anterior part of the cerebellum","ventral part of the medulla oblongata"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the hypothalamus as the basal part of the diencephalon, which belongs to the forebrain.\nIt contains neurosecretory cells that control the pituitary.\nThe midbrain, cerebellum and medulla are other brain regions and do not house the hypothalamus.\nThe dorsal side of the forebrain is where the pineal gland lies, not the hypothalamus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Adrenaline and noradrenaline are called emergency hormones or hormones of fight or flight.\nReason (R): They are rapidly secreted in response to stress and increase alertness, heart beat and rate of respiration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The adrenal medulla secretes catecholamines, which are called emergency or fight-or-flight hormones.\nR is true. These hormones are released rapidly during stress. They raise alertness, heart beat, the strength of heart contraction and the rate of respiration.\nThis rapid release during emergencies is exactly why they get that name, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The thymus lies on the ventral side of the aorta","Melatonin influences the menstrual cycle and metabolism","The pineal gland forms the basal part of the diencephalon and secretes melatonin","Thymosins promote the production of antibodies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the thymus lies behind the sternum on the ventral side of the aorta.\n(2) Correct: melatonin influences the menstrual cycle, metabolism, pigmentation and defense.\n(3) Incorrect: the pineal gland does secrete melatonin, but it lies on the dorsal side of the forebrain. The basal part of the diencephalon is the hypothalamus.\n(4) Correct: thymosins support humoral immunity by promoting antibody production.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following hormones is synthesised by hypothalamic neurons but is stored in and released from the pituitary gland?","image":"","options":["Oxytocin","Prolactin","Melanocyte stimulating hormone","Adrenocorticotrophic hormone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The neurohypophysis (pars nervosa) does not make its own hormones. It stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin.\nBoth are synthesised by hypothalamic neurons and carried along their axons to the posterior pituitary.\nProlactin and ACTH are made in the pars distalis, and MSH is made in the pars intermedia. All three belong to the adenohypophysis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are peptide hormones?\nAtrial natriuretic factor, erythropoietin, gastrin, secretin, cholecystokinin, gastric inhibitory peptide, aldosterone, cortisol","image":"","options":["4","5","6","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes ANF, erythropoietin, gastrin, secretin, CCK and GIP as peptide hormones. That makes 6.\nAldosterone and cortisol are corticoids secreted by the adrenal cortex. They are steroids, not peptides.\nThe total is therefore 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The pancreas is a composite gland because it acts as both an exocrine and an endocrine gland.\nStatement II: The Islets of Langerhans make up about half of the total pancreatic tissue.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT calls the pancreas a composite gland with both exocrine and endocrine roles.\nStatement II is incorrect. A normal human pancreas has about 1 to 2 million islets.\nThese islets form only about 1 to 2 per cent of the pancreatic tissue, not about half.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (action):\nList-I:\n(A) Cortisol\n(B) Aldosterone\n(C) Adrenaline\n(D) Adrenal androgens\nList-II:\n(i) Reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water at renal tubules\n(ii) Growth of axial, pubic and facial hair at puberty\n(iii) Anti-inflammatory reactions and immune suppression\n(iv) Increase in heart beat and strength of contraction","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Cortisol produces anti-inflammatory reactions and suppresses the immune response.\nB-(i): Aldosterone stimulates reabsorption of Na$^+$ and water at the renal tubules.\nC-(iv): Adrenaline increases the heart beat and the strength of heart contraction.\nD-(ii): Small amounts of adrenal androgens support the growth of axial, pubic and facial hair at puberty.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following organs in mammals does not consist of a central medullary region surrounded by a cortical region?","image":"","options":["Ovary","Adrenal","Liver","Kidney"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Kidney: it has an outer cortex and an inner medulla.\nAdrenal gland: it has an outer cortex and an inner medulla.\nOvary: it has an outer cortex containing follicles and an inner medulla.\nLiver: it is made of hepatic lobules and has no cortex-medulla arrangement.\nSo the liver is the organ without a central medulla surrounded by a cortex.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q14)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The neurohypophysis releases oxytocin and vasopressin but does not synthesise them.\nReason (R): Oxytocin and vasopressin are synthesised in the hypothalamus and transported axonally to the neurohypophysis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The pars nervosa only stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin.\nR is true. These hormones are made by hypothalamic neurons and carried along their axons to the neurohypophysis.\nR is the reason the posterior pituitary releases but does not make these hormones, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A rise in blood pressure triggers secretion of atrial natriuretic factor (ANF), which brings the blood pressure down.\nReason (R): ANF causes dilation of blood vessels.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the atrial wall secretes ANF when blood pressure increases, and ANF decreases blood pressure.\nR is true: ANF causes dilation of blood vessels.\nVasodilation is exactly how ANF reduces blood pressure. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"If the thymus of a newborn mammal were surgically removed, which outcome would be expected, based on the role of thymosins?","image":"","options":["Only humoral immunity would be impaired; T-lymphocytes would differentiate normally","Only cell-mediated immunity would be impaired; antibody production would remain fully normal","Cell-mediated immunity would be impaired and antibody production would also decrease","No effect on immunity, since the thymus functions only in old age"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Thymosins from the thymus drive T-lymphocyte differentiation, which underlies cell-mediated immunity.\nThymosins also promote antibody production, which underlies humoral immunity.\nRemoving the thymus therefore impairs both kinds of immunity. Options (1) and (2) each ignore one of these roles.\nThe thymus is actually most active early in life and degenerates in old age, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following conditions is not linked to deficiency of thyroid hormones?","image":"","options":["Cretinism","Goitre","Myxedema","Exophthalmia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Cretinism: caused by hypothyroidism during development, so it is linked to deficiency.\n(2) Goitre: simple goitre results from iodine deficiency, which lowers thyroid hormone, so it is linked to deficiency.\n(3) Myxedema: caused by hypothyroidism in adults, so it is linked to deficiency.\n(4) Exophthalmia: bulging eyes seen in exophthalmic goitre (Graves' disease), a form of hyperthyroidism. It is not caused by deficiency.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 22 (MCQ Q15)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A normal human pancreas contains about 1 to 2 million Islets of Langerhans. What proportion of the pancreatic tissue do these islets represent?","image":"","options":["About 1 to 2 per cent","About 10 to 20 per cent","About 50 per cent","About 80 to 90 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, there are about 1 to 2 million islets in a normal human pancreas.\nTogether they form only 1 to 2 per cent of the pancreatic tissue.\nMost of the pancreas is exocrine tissue, so the larger percentages are wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-186","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":186,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following hormones are produced by the pars distalis of the pituitary?\nGrowth hormone, Prolactin, MSH, TSH, Oxytocin, ACTH, LH, Vasopressin, FSH","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The pars distalis (anterior pituitary) produces six hormones: GH, PRL, TSH, ACTH, LH and FSH.\nMSH is secreted by the pars intermedia.\nOxytocin and vasopressin are made in the hypothalamus and only stored and released by the pars nervosa.\nSo the count is 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-187","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":187,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the thyroid gland are correct?\n(i) Follicular cells synthesise both T$_3$ and T$_4$.\n(ii) Graves' disease is characterised by decreased basal metabolic rate and weight gain.\n(iii) Hypothyroidism during pregnancy can lead to cretinism in the baby.\n(iv) Thyroid hormones support red blood cell formation.\n(v) The two lobes are joined by a thin flap of connective tissue called the isthmus.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Follicular cells make tetraiodothyronine and triiodothyronine.\n(ii) Incorrect: Graves' disease is a form of hyperthyroidism, with increased BMR and weight loss.\n(iii) Correct: It causes stunted growth, mental retardation and deaf-mutism in the baby.\n(iv) Correct: Thyroid hormones support erythropoiesis.\n(v) Correct: The isthmus is a thin connective tissue flap joining the lobes.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-188","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":188,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of insulin on its target cells or on blood?\n(i) Enhanced cellular uptake of glucose\n(ii) Stimulation of glycogenolysis\n(iii) Conversion of glucose to glycogen\n(iv) Stimulation of gluconeogenesis\n(v) Decrease in blood glucose level\n(vi) Reduced cellular utilisation of glucose","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: insulin enhances glucose uptake by hepatocytes and adipocytes.\n(ii) No: glycogenolysis is a glucagon effect.\n(iii) Yes: insulin stimulates glycogenesis.\n(iv) No: gluconeogenesis is stimulated by glucagon.\n(v) Yes: insulin causes hypoglycemia.\n(vi) No: insulin increases glucose utilisation; glucagon reduces it.\nSo three effects belong to insulin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-189","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":189,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The posterior pituitary is under the direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus.\nReason (R): Hypothalamic hormones reach the posterior pituitary only through a portal circulatory system.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The posterior pituitary is under direct neural regulation of the hypothalamus. Oxytocin and vasopressin are transported along axons from hypothalamic neurons to the neurohypophysis.\nR is false. The portal circulation carries releasing and inhibiting hormones to the anterior pituitary, not the posterior pituitary.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-190","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":190,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"The formation of alveoli, the sac-like milk-storing structures of the mammary glands, is stimulated mainly by:","image":"","options":["Testosterone","Progesterone","Oxytocin","Melatonin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Progesterone acts on the mammary glands.\nIt stimulates the formation of alveoli, which store milk, and also stimulates milk secretion.\nOxytocin is a tempting choice, but it causes milk ejection, not alveolus formation.\nTestosterone and melatonin have no such role.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-191","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":191,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (chemical nature):\nList-I\n(a) Cortisol\n(b) Epinephrine\n(c) Thyroxine\n(d) Glucagon\nList-II\n(i) Iodothyronine\n(ii) Peptide hormone\n(iii) Steroid\n(iv) Amino-acid derivative","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Cortisol is a steroid, as are testosterone, estradiol and progesterone, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Epinephrine is an amino-acid derivative, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Thyroxine (T4) belongs to the iodothyronines, so (c)-(i).\n(d) Glucagon is a peptide hormone, as is insulin, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-192","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":192,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A patient passes large volumes of dilute urine and is repeatedly dehydrated. Blood glucose is normal, and secretion of GH, TSH and ACTH is normal. Damage to which structure most likely explains these findings?","image":"","options":["Cells of the pars distalis","Cells of the pars intermedia","Hypothalamic neurons whose axons end in the pars nervosa","Portal blood vessels supplying the anterior pituitary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Normal blood glucose rules out diabetes mellitus. The symptoms fit diabetes insipidus, which is ADH deficiency.\nADH is synthesised by hypothalamic neurons and carried along their axons to the pars nervosa for storage and release. Damage to these neurons would reduce ADH.\nDamage to the pars distalis or the portal vessels would disturb GH, TSH and ACTH, which are normal here.\nThe pars intermedia secretes only MSH.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-193","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":193,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram of a human ovary after ovulation shows a structure labelled X. X has formed from the ruptured graafian follicle. Which hormone is mainly secreted by X?","image":"","options":["Estrogen","Luteinizing hormone","Progesterone","Testosterone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"After ovulation, the ruptured follicle is converted into the corpus luteum, so X is the corpus luteum.\nThe corpus luteum mainly secretes progesterone.\nEstrogen comes mainly from the growing follicles before ovulation.\nLH is a pituitary hormone. It maintains the corpus luteum but is not secreted by it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CHEMICAL-COORD-194","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Coordination and Integration","qno":194,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Coordination and Integration","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (condition) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(a) Cretinism\n(b) Graves' disease\n(c) Iodine-deficiency goitre\n(d) Hypothyroidism in adult women\nList-II\n(i) Irregular menstrual cycle\n(ii) Deaf-mutism and low intelligence quotient\n(iii) Protrusion of eyeballs with weight loss\n(iv) Enlarged thyroid with low hormone output","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): Cretinism, from hypothyroidism in pregnancy, causes deaf-mutism and low IQ in the child.\n(b)-(iii): Graves' disease shows protruding eyeballs, high BMR and weight loss.\n(c)-(iv): Iodine deficiency causes hypothyroidism with an enlarged thyroid.\n(d)-(i): Hypothyroidism in adult women may make the menstrual cycle irregular.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19 · Chemical Coordination and Integration","difficulty":"medium"}]});
